#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Keep the Claude Code session list identical across Claude Desktop accounts.

WHAT IS SYNCED
    Claude Desktop stores one session *index* per account:

        ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude-code-sessions/
            <accountUuid>/<orgUuid>/local_<id>.json

    Each file is a small JSON record (title, working directory, cliSessionId,
    lastActivityAt, ...). The record carries no account or organisation field,
    so it is portable between stores verbatim. This tool copies those records
    between stores, and nothing else.

WHAT IS NEVER TOUCHED
    Conversation content lives in

        ~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<cliSessionId>.jsonl

    which is NOT account-scoped: every account on the machine already reads the
    same transcripts. This tool never reads, writes, copies or backs up a
    transcript. Only the small index files above are ever moved.

HOW IT RESOLVES CONFLICTS
    For each sessionId the copy with the largest `lastActivityAt` wins, and is
    written to every store that lacks it or holds an older one. It is not a
    one-way mirror: every store syncs with every other store.

DELETION RULE -- OPT-IN, AND OFF BY DEFAULT
    A deleted session leaves a marker file named `deleted_<uuid>` whose content
    is a millisecond timestamp.

    BY DEFAULT this tool never removes a record or a marker. It does not
    unlink a record, it does not write or propagate a deletion marker, and it
    does not clear a stale marker. A default run only ever ADDS a record to a
    store that lacks it, or replaces a strictly older copy. Every serious bug
    found in this tool has been in the deletion path, so the default is the
    mode that cannot lose anything; propagating deletions is something you
    ask for.

    A default run still HONOURS each store's own marker: a session a store
    deleted is not pushed back into that store. Since this mode may not clear
    a marker either, it writes no record into a store that holds ANY marker
    for that session, stale or not -- a record sitting next to a marker is the
    combination that can hide the session, and no later default run could
    repair it. So a session you delete in one account stays gone there and
    survives in the others, and this mode never creates that pair itself (a
    pair already on disk is left as it is; --propagate-deletions clears it).

    --propagate-deletions turns the deletion path on. A session then counts as
    deliberately deleted when a marker really exists AND its timestamp is at
    least as new as the winning record's `lastActivityAt`; the record is
    removed from every store and the marker is propagated everywhere. A marker
    OLDER than the record is stale (the session was used again after being
    deleted); it is never propagated, and a stale marker found next to a live
    record is removed, because the app does not produce that combination
    itself and it can hide the session. A marker is copied into other stores
    only when NO store holds a file for that session any more: a file that
    exists but could not be interpreted -- unparseable, a name that is not a
    plain local_<id>, a body naming a different session -- blocks propagation,
    because writing a marker next to a living record is precisely what hides
    the session.

    --no-delete is still accepted, as a deprecated no-op: it now describes the
    default.

    The one thing an applying run still removes is this tool's own temp file
    -- one left behind by a run that was killed mid-write, or the one it has
    just written itself when the last check before the rename says the write
    must not happen. It is named exactly the way its writer names them,
    `.sync-<pid>-local_<id>.json.tmp`, and is neither a record nor a marker.
    Nothing else is swept, whatever its name.

WHEN IN DOUBT, NOTHING HAPPENS
    Every input here can be caught half-written, because the app rewrites these
    files while this job runs. The rule is that an unreadable input means "skip
    this one item this run", never "assume it was empty":

      * a record file that will not parse, or that is not a JSON object, is
        skipped -- as a source and as a destination. An unreadable destination
        is never overwritten and never unlinked, so an older copy can never
        clobber the newest one and a file nobody could read is never deleted.
        A record holding a number that is not finite -- NaN, Infinity, or a
        literal such as 1e999 that overflows to one -- is unreadable in the
        same sense: a copy of it would not be JSON, and the app could not
        parse it. Nothing this tool writes contains such a token.
      * a deletion marker that exists but whose content is not a plausible
        millisecond timestamp (empty, truncated mid-write, garbage) is treated
        as unknown-but-present. The session is left completely alone for that
        run: it is not resurrected, not deleted, and the marker is neither
        copied nor removed. It is reported instead, because guessing either way
        destroys data.
      * a `deleted_<uuid>` entry that is not a plain regular file -- a
        directory, a symlink, a fifo -- is the same case: it cannot be read as
        a marker, but it is not "no marker" either. It is unknown-but-present,
        so that session is left alone in every store (above all, no record is
        written next to it), the entry itself is never touched, and it is
        counted and reported. Index entries are only ever opened as regular
        files, without following a symlink and without blocking on a fifo.
      * a record with no usable `lastActivityAt` is never deleted -- neither
        the winning copy nor another store's copy; if a marker exists for it,
        it is skipped entirely, since the two cannot be compared. "Usable"
        means a millisecond value from 2001 up to a day past this machine's
        clock: a copy stamped further in the future is undatable too, so it
        can never win and never overwrite the genuinely newest copy.
      * a record with no `cliSessionId` names no Claude Code session, so there
        is nothing for another account to resume: it is not copied, and it is
        counted and reported rather than dropped in silence.
      * a DESTINATION whose `lastActivityAt` is present but is not a plausible
        millisecond value cannot be compared with anything either, so that
        store's copy is left exactly as it is -- neither overwritten nor
        unlinked. (A missing or null one stays overwritable: that is a record
        with no opinion, not one we misread.)
      * a DESTINATION whose `sessionId` field names a different session than
        its file name may be that other session's only copy. It is never
        overwritten and never unlinked, just as such a file is never used as a
        source, and it is counted and reported.
      * a directory that cannot be LISTED is not an empty store. The whole
        store is dropped from the run and reported, because a store we cannot
        enumerate cannot be proven to be in the backup either.

    One damaged file can therefore never take the whole run down, and never
    causes a deletion.

BACKUPS
    Before any run that would delete a record, propagate a deletion marker or
    clear a stale one -- and before the very first apply on this machine --
    every store's index files are copied to
    ~/.claude/backups/claude-sessions-<YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS>/. Only
    those index files are ever backed up, never a transcript. Each backup is
    built in a `.partial` directory and renamed into place only when it is
    complete, so a half-written backup is never offered for restoring. A backup
    that does not actually contain every file this run could destroy -- each
    record it would unlink, each stale marker it would clear -- counts as a
    failed backup, and a failed backup means nothing is deleted on that run.
    Any other file it cannot copy (one with no read permission, say) is left
    out with a warning instead of failing the whole backup.

    Retention is not a plain count. The newest 7 are kept, but nothing younger
    than 24 hours is ever pruned, and of the backups taken before a deletion
    (or before a --restore) the NEWEST 3 are kept regardless of how many other
    backups pile up, plus the very first one. The newest ones are the copies
    someone actually reaches for: a wrongly hidden session is noticed days
    after the run that hid it, not months. The first one is the state before
    this tool ever deleted anything. A destructive plan that keeps failing is
    re-planned every minute, and a pure count would let those retries evict
    the only copy of what was deleted. For the same
    reason a new backup is not taken at all when the newest one is recent and
    every index file still has the name, size and modification time it had
    when that backup was written (contents are not compared); the existing one
    is reused as the safety net for this run. (The
    age floor has one limit: past 28 backups the oldest are pruned anyway, so
    a day of churn cannot fill the disk. The protected pre-deletion backups
    are still never among them -- at most 4, which is what the cap is for.)

    --restore-list shows what is available. --restore <timestamp> copies a
    backup back into the matching stores; it clears the deletion markers of the
    sessions it brings back, so the next sync cannot immediately undo it, and
    it first backs up the current state of every store it writes into or
    clears a marker in -- a profile store the sync itself now skips included
    -- so the restore itself is reversible. That safety backup never evicts
    the backup being restored from, and a restore does not start at all if it
    cannot be written or lacks a file the restore would overwrite or clear. A
    store that cannot be listed is left alone, and a profile that no longer
    exists is not recreated. A restore is a
    rollback, so a record that is NEWER on disk than in the backup is replaced
    by the older copy -- before copying anything it says how many records that
    is, and names the first few. It never writes a backed-up marker into a
    store that now holds a live record for that session, and never writes
    through anything at a record's or marker's path that is not a regular file.
    Nothing the backup does not know about is removed.

IMPORTANT: WHEN CHANGES BECOME VISIBLE
    Claude Desktop reads the session list only at startup and on account switch;
    it does not watch the directory. Synced sessions therefore appear after a
    restart or an account switch, not live in an already-open window.

USAGE
    claude-sessions-sync                      dry run, writes no session data
    claude-sessions-sync --apply              synchronize now
    claude-sessions-sync --apply --quiet      log only when something changed
    claude-sessions-sync --apply --propagate-deletions   also apply deletions
                                                         (opt-in)
    claude-sessions-sync --no-delete          deprecated no-op: now the default
    claude-sessions-sync --install [--every 1m]   install the background job
    claude-sessions-sync --uninstall          remove the background job
    claude-sessions-sync --status             interval, deletion mode, recent log
    claude-sessions-sync --apply --wait 20    wait for a running sync instead of skipping
    claude-sessions-sync --restore-list       list available backups
    claude-sessions-sync --restore <stamp>    copy a backup back into the stores

Duration units: s=seconds, m=minutes, h=hours, d=days. "20s", "1m", "1m30s",
"2h", "30d" or a bare number of seconds. Case-insensitive; m means minutes, not
months. launchd throttles anything below 10 seconds, so 10s is the floor.
"""
import argparse
import datetime
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import plistlib
import re
import shutil
import stat as stat_mod
import subprocess
import sys
import time

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
APP = os.path.join(HOME, "Library/Application Support/Claude")
BASE = os.path.join(APP, "claude-code-sessions")
# Extra Claude instances opened by `claude-multi <email>` run with their own
# Electron --user-data-dir. Their session stores are synced too.
PROFILES_ROOT = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude-profiles")
LOGDIR = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude/logs")
LOG = os.path.join(LOGDIR, "claude-sessions-sync.log")
# launchd's StandardErrorPath is a SEPARATE file: launchd holds it open and
# appends tracebacks to it, so this process must never rewrite the same file it
# writes its own lines to.
ERRLOG = os.path.join(LOGDIR, "claude-sessions-sync.err.log")
LOCK = os.path.join(LOGDIR, "claude-sessions-sync.lock")
BACKUP_ROOT = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude/backups")
BACKUP_PREFIX = "claude-sessions-"
PARTIAL_SUFFIX = ".partial"
KEEP_BACKUPS = 7
# Pre-deletion backups protected from pruning: the NEWEST this many, plus the
# very first one. Protecting the oldest three instead meant the snapshots of
# the most recent destructive runs -- the ones a user notices and wants to
# undo -- were the first pre-deletion backups evicted, and so was the safety
# backup every --restore takes. Capped, because an unprunable set must not be
# able to grow without bound: at most KEEP_PRE_DELETE + 1 are protected.
KEEP_PRE_DELETE = 3
# Retention floors on top of the count. A backup is taken before EVERY
# destructive run, and a destructive plan that cannot complete (an unlinkable
# record, a read-only store) is re-planned by the background job every minute,
# so KEEP_BACKUPS alone can be churned through in KEEP_BACKUPS minutes -- which
# would evict exactly the backup holding what the first destructive run
# removed. Nothing younger than MIN_BACKUP_AGE is pruned, and the protected
# pre-deletion backups (see KEEP_PRE_DELETE) are never pruned at all.
MIN_BACKUP_AGE = 24 * 3600
# ... and a stuck destructive plan must not write a new backup every minute
# either: an existing one taken this recently, over an index set whose names,
# sizes and mtimes have not changed since (see backup_fingerprint), is the
# same safety net.
BACKUP_REUSE_AGE = 24 * 3600
# The age floor has to yield to something, or a destructive plan that stays
# stuck for a day while the stores keep changing would keep writing full
# backups that nothing may prune. Above this many, the oldest are pruned even
# when they are young -- but never a protected pre-deletion one, which is the
# copy the floor exists to protect.
HARD_MAX_BACKUPS = 4 * KEEP_BACKUPS
# Bookkeeping files inside a backup directory. Both start with a dot, so
# neither restore() nor restore_list() (which look only at local_*/deleted_*)
# ever treats them as content.
PRE_DELETE_MARK = ".pre-deletion"
# uuid<TAB>email, maintained by bin/claude-multi. Used only to recognise a
# profile directory this tool set manages; never parsed for anything else.
ACCOUNTS_TSV = os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, "accounts.tsv")
# A profile directory that is NOT in that registry has to prove it is still
# live some other way: the APP must have written one of its own files in it
# this recently. See is_live_profile() and profile_activity() for the rule.
PROFILE_LIVE_AGE = 30 * 86400
# Top-level entries of a profile directory that this tool set writes itself,
# so their mtimes say nothing about whether the app still uses the profile:
# the session tree, whose directories every sync write bumps, and the MCP
# config bin/claude-multi may copy in when it creates a profile.
NOT_APP_EVIDENCE = frozenset(("claude-code-sessions", "claude_desktop_config.json"))
FINGERPRINT_FILE = ".fingerprint"
MAIN_LOG = os.path.join(HOME, "Library/Logs/Claude/main.log")
LABEL = "io.github.claude-multi.sync"
PLIST = os.path.join(HOME, "Library/LaunchAgents", LABEL + ".plist")
SCRIPT = os.path.abspath(__file__)
MAX_LOG_LINES = 800
MAX_ERRLOG_BYTES = 256 * 1024
MAIN_LOG_TAIL_BYTES = 256 * 1024
MIN_INTERVAL = 10  # launchd throttles anything shorter anyway

# A deletion marker holds milliseconds. Anything below 10**12 (2001-09-09) is a
# write we caught half-finished, not a real timestamp; anything above 10**14
# (year 5138) is garbage. Both are treated as "unreadable", never as a value.
MIN_MARKER_MS = 10 ** 12
MAX_MARKER_MS = 10 ** 14
# ... and so is anything more than a day ahead of this machine's clock, in a
# record as much as in a marker. 10**14 alone let a copy stamped in the year
# 2445 (a wrong system clock, a damaged value) win every newest-wins
# comparison for ever and be written back over the user's genuinely newest
# copy on every run; a marker stamped like that would delete its session
# again every time it was used. The day of slack absorbs a clock that is
# merely a little fast. Fixed once per process, so every comparison in one
# run uses the same bound.
FUTURE_SLACK_MS = 86400 * 1000
LATEST_MS = min(MAX_MARKER_MS, int(time.time() * 1000) + FUTURE_SLACK_MS)
MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d+)(?:\.\d+)?$")

# Records are published with an atomic rename from a temp file in the same
# directory. The name is hidden, unique per process and does not end in the
# ".json" the app looks for, and stale ones are swept at the start of each
# applying run, so a killed process cannot leave something the app might read.
TMP_PREFIX = ".sync-"
TMP_SUFFIX = ".tmp"
STRAY_TMP_AGE = 300
# The exact shape the writer produces: TMP_PREFIX, the writing pid, the record
# name it stands in for, TMP_SUFFIX. The sweep removes this and nothing else --
# not a `local_*.tmp`, not anything else that merely starts with the prefix --
# because it is the one unlink every applying run does, the default included.
TMP_RE = re.compile(r"^%s\d+-local_[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\.json%s$"
                    % (re.escape(TMP_PREFIX), re.escape(TMP_SUFFIX)))

# Backups and logs contain session titles and working-directory paths.
DIR_MODE = 0o700
FILE_MODE = 0o600

QUIET = False
# --wait: how long to wait for a sync that is already running before giving up.
# 0 keeps the historical behaviour (give up at once). bin/claude-multi passes a
# few seconds when it syncs right before opening a profile: skipping that sync
# because the background job happened to be mid-run would open the profile on
# a list that is one run out of date.
LOCK_WAIT = 0.0

UNITS = {"S": 1, "M": 60, "H": 3600, "D": 86400}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# small helpers
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def parse_interval(text):
    """'20s', '1m', '1m30s', '2h', '30d' or bare seconds ('90') -> seconds.

    Case-insensitive. Anything else is rejected rather than guessed at.
    """
    s = str(text).strip().replace(" ", "").upper()
    if not s:
        raise ValueError("empty duration")
    if s.isdigit():
        total = int(s)
    else:
        parts = re.findall(r"(\d+)\s*([SMHD])", s)
        if not parts or "".join(a + b for a, b in parts) != s:
            raise ValueError(
                "cannot parse duration: %s  (examples: 20s, 1m, 1m30s, 2h, 30d)" % text)
        total = sum(int(n) * UNITS[u] for n, u in parts)
    if total <= 0:
        raise ValueError("duration must be greater than zero")
    return total


def human(sec):
    for unit, name in ((86400, "day"), (3600, "hour"), (60, "minute")):
        if sec % unit == 0 and sec >= unit:
            n = sec // unit
            return "%d %s%s" % (n, name, "" if n == 1 else "s")
    return "%d second%s" % (sec, "" if sec == 1 else "s")


def ms(value):
    """A millisecond timestamp out of an arbitrary JSON value; 0 when unusable.

    Records are written by other programs, so `lastActivityAt` may be missing,
    null, a string or something else entirely. Everything that is not a usable
    number becomes 0, which callers must read as "unknown" -- never as "old".

    A number that IS there but cannot be milliseconds is unusable too, and this
    is the same window read_marker() enforces on markers. Seconds (1.7e9) would
    compare older than every marker and make each one look decisive; micro- or
    nanoseconds, or a 25-digit string from a half-written file, would win every
    newest-wins comparison forever and be copied over the genuinely newest copy
    in every store. Both become 0 = unknown, which plan() handles by leaving
    the session alone rather than guessing. The upper end of the window is
    LATEST_MS -- a day past this machine's clock -- and not merely the year
    5138: a copy stamped far in the future is undatable in exactly the same
    way, so it never wins a comparison and, as a destination, is left alone.
    """
    if isinstance(value, bool):
        v = 0
    elif isinstance(value, int):
        v = value
    elif isinstance(value, float):
        try:
            v = int(value)  # NaN and the infinities raise here
        except (ValueError, OverflowError):
            v = 0
    elif isinstance(value, str):
        m = MARKER_RE.match(value.strip())
        try:
            v = int(m.group(1)) if m else 0
        except ValueError:
            v = 0
    else:
        v = 0
    return v if MIN_MARKER_MS <= v <= LATEST_MS else 0


def chmod_quiet(path, mode):
    try:
        os.chmod(path, mode)
    except OSError:
        pass


def log(msg, force=False):
    # --quiet means "say nothing unless something happened": in quiet mode a
    # non-forced message is not printed AND not written, otherwise the
    # background job would add a line to the log on every single run.
    if QUIET and not force:
        return
    line = "%s  %s" % (datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), msg)
    if not QUIET or force:
        print(line if QUIET else msg)
    try:
        os.makedirs(LOGDIR, exist_ok=True)
        chmod_quiet(LOGDIR, DIR_MODE)
        # UTF-8 whatever the locale: a path in the line may not be ASCII.
        with open(LOG, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(line + "\n")
        chmod_quiet(LOG, FILE_MODE)
    except Exception:
        pass


_WARNED = set()


def warn_once(key, msg):
    """Log a diagnosis about the machine's layout at most once per run.

    Not forced: the background job runs every minute and a permanently parked
    profile directory would otherwise add a line to the log 1440 times a day.
    An interactive run (and therefore the dry run) prints it.
    """
    if key in _WARNED:
        return
    _WARNED.add(key)
    log(msg)


def trim_log():
    """Keep both logs bounded. Called at the end of EVERY run, including runs
    that changed nothing: a job that only ever fails still appends to the error
    log, and that is exactly the case the cap exists for."""
    try:
        with open(LOG, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            lines = fh.readlines()
        if len(lines) > MAX_LOG_LINES:
            with open(LOG, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.writelines(lines[-MAX_LOG_LINES // 2:])
            chmod_quiet(LOG, FILE_MODE)
    except Exception:
        pass
    # ERRLOG belongs to launchd (StandardErrorPath). We never write lines to
    # it, we only cap its size; launchd appends, so it cannot lose a line to a
    # hole, at worst a line written during this rewrite is dropped.
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(ERRLOG)
        if size > MAX_ERRLOG_BYTES:
            with open(ERRLOG, "rb") as fh:
                fh.seek(size - MAX_ERRLOG_BYTES // 2)
                tail = fh.read()
            with open(ERRLOG, "wb") as fh:
                fh.write(tail)
            chmod_quiet(ERRLOG, FILE_MODE)
    except Exception:
        pass


class Lock:
    """Single-run lock held with flock(2).

    The kernel drops the lock when this process exits however it exits, so
    there is no staleness heuristic to tune and no way to release -- or delete
    -- a lock that belongs to another run. The lock FILE is never unlinked for
    the same reason: the lock is the open descriptor, not the directory entry.
    """

    fd = None

    def __enter__(self):
        os.makedirs(LOGDIR, exist_ok=True)
        chmod_quiet(LOGDIR, DIR_MODE)
        fd = os.open(LOCK, os.O_CREAT | os.O_RDWR, FILE_MODE)
        # Monotonic, not the wall clock: a clock stepped backwards during the
        # wait would stretch it past the cap, a forward step would cut it short.
        deadline = time.monotonic() + max(0.0, LOCK_WAIT)
        while True:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                locked = True
                break
            except OSError:
                if time.monotonic() < deadline:
                    time.sleep(0.2)
                    continue
                locked = False
                break
        if not locked:
            other = ""
            try:
                other = os.read(fd, 32).decode("utf-8", "ignore").strip()
            except OSError:
                pass
            try:
                os.close(fd)
            except OSError:
                pass
            # Exit 0: colliding with the every-minute background job is normal
            # and must not show up as a failed run in `launchctl print`. Say so
            # out loud in an interactive run, though, so it cannot be mistaken
            # for "synced, nothing to do". The background job stays silent.
            if not QUIET:
                log("another run is already in progress%s -- nothing was done."
                    % (" (pid %s)" % other if other else ""))
            raise SystemExit(0)
        self.fd = fd
        try:
            os.ftruncate(fd, 0)
            os.write(fd, str(os.getpid()).encode())
        except OSError:
            pass
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        if self.fd is None:
            return
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            os.close(self.fd)
        except OSError:
            pass
        self.fd = None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# store discovery
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

_ACTIVE_STORE_CACHE = ()  # () = not computed yet, (value,) = computed

# The main app's config.json is a few kilobytes. The cap only bounds what is
# read when something else sits at that path; a file larger than this is not
# parsed at all.
CONFIG_MAX_BYTES = 4 * 1024 * 1024
# An initialization line in the desktop app's main.log. The org id is anchored
# at its end: a longer token ("<id>/../x", "<id>.old", "<id>_2") must not be
# accepted by its hexadecimal prefix. A full stop that merely ends the
# sentence does not count as part of the token.
INIT_LINE_RE = re.compile(
    r"Initialization succeeded.*?accountId=([0-9a-fA-F-]+),\s*"
    r"orgId=([0-9a-fA-F-]+)(?![0-9A-Za-z_/-]|\.[0-9A-Za-z_./-])")
# Every main.log line starts with a local-time stamp.
LOG_STAMP_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d{4})-(\d\d)-(\d\d) (\d\d):(\d\d):(\d\d)")
# How old the main account's newest initialization line has to be before the
# store it names is adopted. See active_store().
ACTIVE_MIN_AGE = 10


def main_account():
    """The account the MAIN app (the default user-data directory) is signed in
    to, from its own config.json, lower-cased; None when it cannot be read.

    Read through read_regular(): a fifo at that path must not stall the run
    while it holds the lock. A symlinked config.json is followed -- the file
    is only ever read."""
    try:
        raw = read_regular(os.path.join(APP, "config.json"),
                           CONFIG_MAX_BYTES + 1, follow=True)
        if raw is None or len(raw) > CONFIG_MAX_BYTES:
            return None
        v = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8")).get("lastKnownAccountUuid")
        if isinstance(v, str) and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-fA-F-]{8,64}", v):
            return v.lower()
    except Exception:
        pass
    return None


def log_stamp(line):
    """Seconds since the epoch for the local-time stamp a main.log line starts
    with, or None when the line has no stamp that can be read as a real local
    time. In the hour a clock is set back the same stamp names two moments;
    the later one is returned, so such a line can only look younger than it
    is, never older."""
    m = LOG_STAMP_RE.match(line)
    if not m:
        return None
    want = tuple(int(g) for g in m.groups())
    best = None
    for dst in (0, 1):
        try:
            t = time.mktime(want + (0, 0, dst))
            back = tuple(time.localtime(t)[:6])
        except (ValueError, OverflowError, OSError):
            continue
        if back == want and (best is None or t > best):
            best = t
    return best


def active_store():
    """The <accountUuid>/<orgUuid> directory the MAIN app is using right now.

    It exists for one case: a main app freshly signed in to an account whose
    store is still empty, which the "has records" rule would never pick up.
    (Profiles do not need it; their stores are found by listing, empty or not.)
    A store that holds records is discovered without it, so every doubt here
    is resolved by adopting nothing: the cost is a short delay before a fresh
    store is first filled, never a missed session.

    The org comes from the desktop app's main.log, and that log is SHARED:
    every instance started with --user-data-dir appends to the same file. So
    the last initialization line is not necessarily the main app's, and during
    an account switch the app logs a transient line pairing one account with
    the other account's org, a couple of seconds before the line for the pair
    it settles on. Trusting the last line blindly mapped a profile's account,
    or that transient pair, onto an empty directory under the main tree and
    filled it with a copy of every session that no account ever reads.

    So the candidate is the NEWEST initialization line for the account named
    in the main app's own config.json, and it is adopted only when both hold:

      * it is at least ACTIVE_MIN_AGE seconds old, by its own time stamp. The
        background job is started by the very config.json rewrite that
        accompanies a switch, i.e. inside the window in which the transient
        line can be the newest one. A line whose stamp cannot be read, or
        lies in the future, is not trusted;
      * no line for a DIFFERENT account names the same org after it. That is
        what the settled line does to a transient one: the org turned out to
        belong to the other account.

    When either fails nothing is adopted -- there is no falling back to an
    older line. No readable account, or no line for it, means no active store
    either: nothing is guessed. What this cannot tell apart is a transient
    line that stays the account's newest for longer than that with no later
    claim on its org; the log then reads exactly like a completed sign-in.

    main.log routinely reaches tens of megabytes and this job runs every
    minute, so only the tail is read, and the answer is computed once per
    process. It is read through read_regular(), like config.json, so a fifo
    at that path cannot stall the run."""
    global _ACTIVE_STORE_CACHE
    if _ACTIVE_STORE_CACHE:
        return _ACTIVE_STORE_CACHE[0]
    found = None
    acct = main_account()
    try:
        raw = None
        if acct:
            raw = read_regular(MAIN_LOG, MAIN_LOG_TAIL_BYTES, follow=True,
                               tail=True)
        if raw is not None:
            claimed = set()  # orgs named by a later line for another account
            for line in reversed(raw.decode("utf-8", "ignore").splitlines()):
                m = INIT_LINE_RE.search(line)
                if not m:
                    continue
                if m.group(1).lower() != acct:
                    claimed.add(m.group(2).lower())
                    continue
                stamp = log_stamp(line)
                if (stamp is not None
                        and time.time() - stamp >= ACTIVE_MIN_AGE
                        and m.group(2).lower() not in claimed):
                    p = os.path.join(BASE, m.group(1), m.group(2))
                    if os.path.isdir(p):
                        found = p
                break
    except Exception:
        found = None
    _ACTIVE_STORE_CACHE = (found,)
    return found


# Words people append when they park a copy of a directory, plus whatever
# stamp they add after them ("...bak-20260101", "... copy 2", "...old.save").
PARKED_WORDS = ("bak", "backup", "backups", "old", "orig", "original", "save",
                "saved", "copy", "prev", "previous", "disabled", "unused",
                "archive", "archived", "snapshot")
# Anchored at the END and only after a separator, NOT "anywhere in the name":
# a profile is named after an e-mail address, and one legitimately called
# something like x.bak@example.com must keep syncing -- bin/claude-multi makes
# the same distinction for the same reason.
PARKED_RE = re.compile(
    r"[.\-_ ](?P<word>%s)(?:[.\-_ ][0-9A-Za-z]{1,20})*$"
    % "|".join(PARKED_WORDS),
    re.IGNORECASE)
# A complete address (local part, '@', a domain ending in a TLD-shaped label),
# and the rest of a domain after one of its labels. See is_snapshot_name().
ADDRESS_RE = re.compile(
    r"^[^@\s]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$")
DOMAIN_TAIL_RE = re.compile(r"^(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$")


def is_snapshot_name(name):
    """True for a name that is a parked copy rather than something live.

    The same test is applied to profile, account and org directories:
    "work@example.com.bak-20260101", "work@example.com copy 2",
    "personal@example.com.old", a trailing "~". Only ".bak" used to be
    recognised, so every other way of parking a directory was still folded
    into the sync -- and into the global deletion maximum.

    In a name holding an address, a parked word that is merely a label of the
    DOMAIN does not count: me@mail.archive.example and dev@team.snapshot.example
    are live profiles. The word counts when a complete address comes before it
    (work@example.com.old), or when what follows it is not the rest of a
    domain (me@mail.bak-2026, or a later label that is itself a parked word).
    In doubt it stays a parked copy: skipping a live profile is reported,
    syncing into a snapshot is not recoverable.
    """
    if name.endswith("~"):
        return True
    if "@" not in name:
        return bool(PARKED_RE.search(name))
    for i in range(len(name)):
        m = PARKED_RE.match(name, i)
        if not m:
            continue
        tail = name[m.end("word"):]
        if (ADDRESS_RE.match(name[:i]) or name[i] not in ".-"
                or not DOMAIN_TAIL_RE.match(tail)
                or any(lbl.lower() in PARKED_WORDS for lbl in tail.split("."))):
            return True
    return False


_REGISTERED_CACHE = ()


def registered_profiles():
    """The profile directory names bin/claude-multi knows about.

    accounts.tsv is `uuid<TAB>name`, written by `claude-multi list` (and by
    `setup`, which ends by running it), which is where bin/claude-multi
    learns which account each e-mail-named, signed-in profile holds (opening
    a profile does not write it); the name column is
    the directory name under ~/.claude-profiles. Read once per process, and
    treated as additive evidence only: a missing or unreadable registry means
    "no extra confidence", never "nothing is live".
    """
    global _REGISTERED_CACHE
    if _REGISTERED_CACHE:
        return _REGISTERED_CACHE[0]
    names = set()
    try:
        with open(ACCOUNTS_TSV, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh.read(256 * 1024).splitlines():
                parts = line.split("\t")
                if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1].strip():
                    names.add(parts[1].strip())
    except OSError:
        pass
    _REGISTERED_CACHE = (names,)
    return names


def profile_activity(pdir):
    """Newest mtime of anything the APP itself writes in a profile directory.

    Only evidence this tool cannot manufacture counts. Every record this tool
    writes lands in claude-code-sessions/ with rename(2), which bumps those
    directories' mtimes -- so measuring them, as this once did, meant the
    tool's own writes kept an abandoned profile "written to recently" for
    ever, and PROFILE_LIVE_AGE could never demote it once the first sync had
    written into it.

    What is measured instead is the profile's own top-level app state:
    config.json, Local State, Preferences, Cookies, Network Persistent State
    and the rest of the Electron user-data entries, which a running instance
    rewrites and which nothing in this tool set ever touches. NOT_APP_EVIDENCE
    names the two top-level entries this tool set does write; hidden names
    (.DS_Store and the like) are not the app's either. lstat, so a symlink
    cannot lend the profile another file's age, and not the profile
    directory's own mtime, which creating the session tree inside it bumps.
    """
    newest = 0.0
    for name in scan_names(pdir):
        if name.startswith(".") or name in NOT_APP_EVIDENCE:
            continue
        try:
            newest = max(newest, os.lstat(os.path.join(pdir, name)).st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            continue
    return newest


def is_live_profile(name):
    """(is_live, reason, remedy) for ~/.claude-profiles/<name>.

    THE RULE -- a profile directory is a live store only when all of:

      1. the name is not hidden and does not look like a parked copy
         (see is_snapshot_name);
      2. the directory really is an Electron user-data directory: it holds the
         app's own config.json, which is what bin/claude-multi reads to find
         out which account a profile is signed in to. A directory that merely
         contains a claude-code-sessions tree someone copied out of a profile
         is not a profile;
      3. it is registered in ~/.claude-profiles/accounts.tsv -- which
         `claude-multi list` refreshes from every e-mail-named, signed-in
         profile; opening a profile does NOT write it -- OR the app itself has
         written one of its own files in it within PROFILE_LIVE_AGE (see
         profile_activity(): this tool's own writes do not count).

    Anything else is a frozen copy as far as this tool is concerned. Its
    deletion markers are old decisions the live stores may already have
    undone, so folding them into the global maximum would delete a session
    that is alive everywhere; and writing records into what the user believes
    is a snapshot destroys the very thing they kept it for. Such a directory
    is therefore not read, not counted and never written to, and the run says
    so once, naming it and the remedy. An unregistered profile that has
    simply been idle for a month comes back as soon as the app writes to it
    again -- open it once with `claude-multi <email>` -- and `claude-multi list`
    registers it in accounts.tsv when its name is an e-mail address and it is
    signed in, after which its age no longer matters.
    """
    if name.startswith("."):
        return False, "hidden directory", ""
    if is_snapshot_name(name):
        return False, "the name looks like a parked copy", ""
    pdir = os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, name)
    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(pdir, "config.json")):
        return (False, "no config.json, so it is not an app profile directory",
                "")
    if name in registered_profiles():
        return True, "registered in accounts.tsv", ""
    age = time.time() - profile_activity(pdir)
    if age > PROFILE_LIVE_AGE:
        return (False,
                "not in accounts.tsv, and the app has not written to it for "
                "%d days" % int(age // 86400),
                "if you still use it, run `claude-multi list` to register it "
                "(an e-mail-named, signed-in profile), or open it once")
    return True, "the app wrote to it recently", ""


def bases():
    """The main profile's store root plus every extra profile's store root.

    A parked copy of a profile (`personal.bak-20260101/`) is skipped here, not
    only at the account and org level below it. Such a copy is frozen: its
    deletion markers are old decisions the live stores may already have undone,
    and folding them into the global maximum would delete a session that is
    alive everywhere -- while the sync wrote records into what the user
    believes is a snapshot.
    """
    out = [BASE]
    names = list_dir(PROFILES_ROOT)
    if names is None:
        # Missing is normal (no extra profiles). Present but unlistable means
        # the extra profiles are simply not synced this run, which is the safe
        # direction -- nothing there is read, written or counted.
        if os.path.exists(PROFILES_ROOT):
            warn_once("profiles-root", "WARNING %s cannot be listed, so no "
                      "profile is synced this run" % PROFILES_ROOT)
        return out
    for name in names:
        p = os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, name, "claude-code-sessions")
        if not os.path.isdir(p):
            continue
        live, why, remedy = is_live_profile(name)
        if not live:
            warn_once("profile:" + name,
                      "skipping %s (%s): it is not read, not written, and its "
                      "deletion markers are ignored%s"
                      % (os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, name), why,
                         " -- " + remedy if remedy else ""))
            continue
        out.append(p)
    return out


def label(p):
    """Readable name for reports: 'main:<acct8>' or '<profile>:<acct8>'."""
    acct = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(p))[:8]
    who = "main"
    if p.startswith(PROFILES_ROOT + os.sep):
        who = p[len(PROFILES_ROOT) + 1:].split(os.sep)[0]
    return "%s:%s" % (who, acct)


# A record's file NAME is its identity (see scan()), so every name that is
# treated as a record has to be a plain `local_<id>` with nothing in it that
# could travel into a path: no separator, no "..", nothing to interpret.
RECORD_STEM_RE = re.compile(r"^local_[A-Za-z0-9_-]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+)*$")


def is_record_name(name):
    """True for a session record the app actually reads.

    ".bak" is excluded: the desktop app keeps its own `local_<id>.bak.json`
    copies next to the live records. They sort before the live name, so on
    equal timestamps a stale snapshot would win and be written over every
    store, and one without a sessionId of its own would be propagated as a
    phantom session that no deleted_<id> marker can ever match. count_sessions()
    in bin/claude-multi excludes the same names for the same reason.
    """
    return (name.startswith("local_") and name.endswith(".json")
            and ".bak" not in name)


def stat_ident(path):
    """(inode, size, mtime_ns) for a file, or None when it is not there.

    This is the identity a destination had when the plan was made. Everything
    destructive re-checks it immediately before acting, because minutes can
    pass between plan and apply (a stray-temp sweep and a full backup run in
    between) and the app writes to the live store the whole time.

    lstat: the identity is that of the directory entry itself, so a symlink
    swapped in after the plan does not pass for the file it points at.
    """
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return (st.st_ino, st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns)


def list_dir(path):
    """Sorted entry names of a directory, or None when it cannot be LISTED.

    None is not "empty", and the difference decides whether data can be lost.
    A directory with search but no read permission -- or one replaced while
    the run was thinking -- still answers os.path.exists() and open(), so a
    caller that reads an empty listing as "this store holds nothing" will
    happily write into it, unlink from it, and believe a backup that copied
    none of it. Every caller that is about to write, delete or back up must
    therefore handle None by dropping the directory from the run.

    os.scandir rather than glob: a profile name or a HOME containing '[' turns
    a glob pattern into a character class and silently matches nothing."""
    try:
        with os.scandir(path) as it:
            return sorted(e.name for e in it)
    except OSError:
        return None


def scan_names(path):
    """Sorted entry names; [] when the directory cannot be listed.

    Only for callers where "empty" and "unreadable" lead to the same harmless
    decision: discovering candidate stores (an unlistable one is simply not
    discovered) and sweeping our own temp files. Anything that decides about
    deleting, overwriting or backing up uses list_dir() and handles None."""
    names = list_dir(path)
    return [] if names is None else names


def store_contents(path):
    """(has_record, has_marker) for a candidate store directory."""
    has_rec = has_mark = False
    for name in scan_names(path):
        if is_record_name(name):
            has_rec = True
        elif name.startswith("deleted_"):
            has_mark = True
        if has_rec and has_mark:
            break
    return has_rec, has_mark


def record_count(path):
    return sum(1 for name in scan_names(path) if is_record_name(name))


def stores():
    """Stores to sync: directories holding session records or deletion markers,
    plus every profile directory even when empty, plus whatever the app is
    using right now."""
    out = []
    for base in bases():
        if not os.path.isdir(base):
            continue
        # A freshly signed-in profile has an EMPTY store directory. If we
        # required records there it would never get populated and that profile
        # would show an empty sidebar forever. The main store, on the other
        # hand, accumulates empty cross directories from account switches --
        # those are not stores, so there we keep the "has content" condition.
        # A directory holding only deletion markers IS content: its markers
        # must be read, and it must stay eligible to be repopulated.
        is_profile = base.startswith(PROFILES_ROOT + os.sep)
        accts = list_dir(base)
        if accts is None:
            # Not discovered is safe -- an undiscovered store is never read,
            # written or deleted from -- but it is not silent: a sidebar that
            # stops being synced has to have a visible reason.
            warn_once("base:" + base, "WARNING %s cannot be listed, so no "
                      "store under it is synced this run" % base)
            continue
        for acct in accts:
            ap = os.path.join(base, acct)
            if not os.path.isdir(ap) or is_snapshot_name(acct):
                continue
            orgs = list_dir(ap)
            if orgs is None:
                warn_once("acct:" + ap, "WARNING %s cannot be listed, so no "
                          "store under it is synced this run" % ap)
                continue
            for org in orgs:
                op = os.path.join(ap, org)
                if not os.path.isdir(op) or is_snapshot_name(org):
                    continue
                if not is_profile and list_dir(op) is None:
                    # store_contents() cannot tell this from an empty cross
                    # directory, so it would drop out without a word. Not
                    # discovered is the safe direction, but say so. (The
                    # active store is appended below whatever happens here,
                    # and scan() reports it.)
                    if not same_dir(op, active_store()):
                        warn_once("store:" + op, "WARNING %s cannot be listed, "
                                  "so it is not synced this run" % op)
                    continue
                if is_profile or any(store_contents(op)):
                    out.append(op)
    # By identity, not by spelling: the active store's path is built from the
    # log's own spelling of the two ids, and on a case-insensitive volume
    # another letter case names the very directory that was listed above.
    # Compared as strings, that one store was in the run twice.
    act = active_store()
    if act and not any(same_dir(act, p) for p in out):
        out.append(act)
    return out


def same_dir(a, b):
    """True when both paths name the same directory entry on disk (device and
    inode), however each is spelled. False when either is missing or None."""
    if not a or not b:
        return False
    try:
        return os.path.samefile(a, b)
    except OSError:
        return False


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# reading index files
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def read_regular(path, limit=-1, follow=False, tail=False):
    """The bytes of `path` when it is a plain regular file, else None.

    Index files live in directories other programs write to, so the name
    alone promises nothing about what is behind it. A fifo would block a
    plain open() for ever -- holding the run lock, so every later run would
    report "already in progress" and the background job would stop syncing
    -- and a symlink would make this tool read, back up or overwrite a file
    outside the store. So: O_NOFOLLOW (a symlink fails to open), O_NONBLOCK
    (a fifo opens at once instead of waiting for a writer), and fstat on the
    descriptor that was actually opened, which must be a regular file.

    The two options are for the app's own config.json and main.log, which are
    only ever read and are not index files. `follow` leaves O_NOFOLLOW out, so
    a symlinked one keeps working -- what it leads to must still be a regular
    file. `tail` returns the LAST `limit` bytes instead of the first.
    """
    flags = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK
    if not follow:
        flags |= getattr(os, "O_NOFOLLOW", 0)
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, flags)
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        st = os.fstat(fd)
        if not stat_mod.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            return None
        if tail and limit > 0:
            os.lseek(fd, max(0, st.st_size - limit), os.SEEK_SET)
        chunks, left = [], limit
        while left != 0:
            chunk = os.read(fd, 65536 if left < 0 else min(left, 65536))
            if not chunk:
                break
            chunks.append(chunk)
            if left > 0:
                left -= len(chunk)
        return b"".join(chunks)
    except OSError:
        return None
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def is_regular_entry(path):
    """True when `path` itself (not what a symlink points at) is a regular file."""
    try:
        return stat_mod.S_ISREG(os.lstat(path).st_mode)
    except OSError:
        return False


def load_record(path):
    """The JSON object in a record file, or None when it cannot be used.

    None means "unknown", never "empty": the caller must skip the file, not
    treat it as a record with no fields. A record caught mid-write fails to
    parse, a file holding a valid JSON non-object (null, a list, a number)
    would raise later on .get(), and an entry that is not a regular file (see
    read_regular) cannot be a record -- all of them land here.

    So does a record holding a number that is not finite: a bare NaN or
    Infinity, or a literal such as 1e999 that overflows to one. Python reads
    those and would write them back out as NaN / Infinity, which is not JSON
    -- the app's parser rejects the whole file -- so copying such a record
    would replace a destination the app can read with one it cannot. The app
    never writes one; a file that holds one is treated like any other record
    that cannot be read."""
    raw = read_regular(path)
    if raw is None:
        return None
    try:
        d = json.loads(raw, parse_float=_finite_float,
                       parse_constant=_not_json)
    except Exception:
        return None
    if not isinstance(d, dict):
        return None
    return d


def _finite_float(text):
    """json parse_float hook: a float, or ValueError when the literal
    overflows to an infinity."""
    v = float(text)
    if v != v or v in (float("inf"), float("-inf")):
        raise ValueError("number is not finite: %s" % text)
    return v


def _not_json(name):
    """json parse_constant hook: NaN, Infinity and -Infinity are not JSON."""
    raise ValueError("not a JSON value: %s" % name)


def names_other_session(rec, sid):
    """True when a record's `sessionId` field names a session other than
    `sid`, the one its file name says it is (see scan())."""
    inner = rec.get("sessionId")
    return isinstance(inner, str) and bool(inner) and inner != sid


def read_marker(path):
    """(timestamp, ok) for a deletion marker.

    ok is False when the file exists but its content is not a plausible
    millisecond timestamp -- empty, truncated mid-write ('17893'), garbage,
    or more than a day in this machine's future (see LATEST_MS).
    Those must never be folded in as 0: 0 compares older than every record and
    would resurrect a session the user just deleted. An entry that is not a
    regular file is not readable as a marker either (see read_regular)."""
    data = read_regular(path, 64)
    if data is None:
        return 0, False
    raw = data.decode("utf-8", "ignore").strip()
    m = MARKER_RE.match(raw)
    if not m:
        return 0, False
    try:
        t = int(m.group(1))
    except ValueError:
        return 0, False
    if not (MIN_MARKER_MS <= t <= LATEST_MS):
        return 0, False
    return t, True


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scan / plan / apply
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def scan(paths):
    """recs:       sid -> (newest record, source path)
       dels:       sid -> newest usable deletion timestamp across all stores
       local_dels: (store, sid) -> that store's own usable deletion timestamp
       unclear:    sids that have a marker somewhere which cannot be read
       seen:       store -> the index file names that store held during the scan
       sskip:      counts of record files deliberately not used as a source
       bad:        stores whose directory could not be LISTED at all

    A session id here is the record's FILE NAME stem and nothing else. The
    name is what the app looks a session up by, it is what the marker files
    are named after, and it is what every destination path in plan() is built
    from -- so keying on the `sessionId` field inside the file would let a
    record whose body disagrees with its own name be written over a DIFFERENT
    session's file in every store, destroying that session's only copy. The
    field is therefore a consistency check only: when it is present, is a
    string and contradicts the name, the file is ambiguous and is skipped as a
    source (and counted), exactly like a record that will not parse.

    A store whose directory cannot be LISTED is not an empty store: it
    contributes nothing at all -- no record, no marker, not even a `seen`
    entry -- and comes back in `bad` so the caller can drop it from the run
    before anything is planned for it. Returning [] for it would be actively
    dangerous: os.path.exists() and open() still work on a directory that has
    search but not read permission, so plan() would go on writing into it and
    unlinking from it while the backup, which can only copy what it can
    enumerate, silently covered none of it.

    `seen` is also what makes marker propagation safe: it is every index file
    name the store really held, including the ones that were NOT usable as a
    source. A record can drop out of `recs` for several non-fatal reasons, and
    "not in recs" must never be read as "no such file anywhere".

    Only usable values go into dels/local_dels, so a timestamp of 0 there
    always means "no marker in that store", never "a marker we could not
    read" -- that case is carried by `unclear` instead. Any file that cannot
    be parsed is skipped; no single damaged file may abort the run.

    A `deleted_<uuid>` ENTRY that is not a regular file -- a directory, a
    symlink (dangling or not), a fifo -- is exactly such a marker: something
    is there under the marker's name and it cannot be read as one. It goes
    into `unclear` like unreadable content does, and into `here`, and is
    counted. Skipping it as "not a marker at all" is what let the default
    mode write a record next to it, which is the one pair that mode promises
    never to create.
    """
    recs, dels, local_dels, unclear = {}, {}, {}, set()
    seen, bad = {}, []
    sskip = {"name": 0, "sid": 0, "clock": 0, "unreadable": 0, "nocli": 0,
             "markertype": 0}
    for p in paths:
        names = list_dir(p)
        if names is None:
            bad.append(p)
            continue
        here = set()
        for b in names:
            f = os.path.join(p, b)
            if b.startswith("deleted_"):
                here.add(b)
                sid = "local_" + b[len("deleted_"):]
                if not is_regular_entry(f):
                    # Present, and not readable as a marker: unknown, never
                    # "absent". The session is left alone everywhere.
                    unclear.add(sid)
                    sskip["markertype"] += 1
                    continue
                t, ok = read_marker(f)
                if not ok:
                    unclear.add(sid)
                    continue
                dels[sid] = max(dels.get(sid, 0), t)
                local_dels[(p, sid)] = max(local_dels.get((p, sid), 0), t)
            elif is_record_name(b):
                here.add(b)
                sid = b[:-len(".json")]
                if not RECORD_STEM_RE.match(sid):
                    # Not a plain local_<id>. It would be joined onto every
                    # store path, so it is never a source and never a target.
                    # It stays in `here`, though: the file exists, so no
                    # deletion marker may be propagated for that session.
                    sskip["name"] += 1
                    continue
                d = load_record(f)
                if d is None:
                    # The app may be mid-write; picked up on the next run.
                    # Counted, because the docs promise every file that could
                    # not be read is reported.
                    sskip["unreadable"] += 1
                    continue
                if not d.get("cliSessionId"):
                    # No Claude Code session behind this record: there is
                    # nothing for another account to resume. Not an error, but
                    # it is reported, or a session that never propagates has
                    # no visible reason why.
                    sskip["nocli"] += 1
                    continue
                inner = d.get("sessionId")
                if isinstance(inner, str) and inner and inner != sid:
                    # Body and name name two different sessions. Which one this
                    # file is cannot be decided here, and guessing writes it
                    # over the other one everywhere. Leave it alone entirely.
                    sskip["sid"] += 1
                    continue
                la = ms(d.get("lastActivityAt"))
                if la <= 0 and d.get("lastActivityAt") is not None:
                    # Present but not a plausible millisecond value. Kept as a
                    # source for stores that have no copy at all, but it can
                    # never win a comparison, so it can never overwrite one.
                    sskip["clock"] += 1
                cur = recs.get(sid)
                if not cur or la > ms(cur[0].get("lastActivityAt")):
                    recs[sid] = (d, f)
        seen[p] = here
    return recs, dels, local_dels, unclear, seen, sskip, bad

def plan(paths, recs, dels, local_dels, unclear, seen, do_delete):
    """Work out what to write, remove, mark and clear.

    Returns (writes, removes, marks, stale, skipped), where `skipped` counts
    the items deliberately left alone because something could not be read.

    `do_delete` is False unless --propagate-deletions was given. In that
    default mode `removes`, `marks` and `stale` all come back empty: this
    function may then only add a record to a store that lacks it, or replace a
    strictly older copy, and it leaves any store that holds a marker for the
    session completely alone.

    Every destination that is going to be written or unlinked is recorded with
    the identity it had while it was being read (see stat_ident). The apply
    step re-checks that identity right before it acts: the two are separated by
    a stray-temp sweep and a full backup, and the app keeps writing to the
    store it has open the whole time.
    """
    writes, removes, marks, stale = [], [], [], []
    skipped = {"marker": 0, "dest": 0, "destclock": 0, "destsid": 0,
               "timestamp": 0, "changed": 0, "propagate": 0, "pair": 0}

    # Every session id that some store holds a `local_<sid>.json` file for,
    # whatever scan() then decided to do with that file. `recs` is NOT that
    # set: a record drops out of it when its stem is not a plain local_<id>,
    # when its body names a different session, when it will not parse, or
    # when it has no cliSessionId. Marker propagation below consults THIS set,
    # because a marker written next to a record that is on disk is exactly the
    # pair that hides a session -- and nothing ever clears it again, since the
    # stale-marker pass only iterates `recs`.
    on_disk = set()
    for names in seen.values():
        for n in names:
            if n.startswith("local_") and n.endswith(".json"):
                on_disk.add(n[:-len(".json")])
    # sid -> stores where a record for a dead session is still on disk after
    # this plan, because its removal had to be postponed. No marker may be
    # written into those either.
    held = {}

    for sid, (best, src) in sorted(recs.items()):
        la = ms(best.get("lastActivityAt"))
        mt = dels.get(sid)

        if sid in unclear:
            # A marker for this session exists somewhere but cannot be read.
            # Unknown-but-present: copying the record back would resurrect a
            # session the user just deleted, and deleting on a guess would
            # destroy a live one. Leave the session entirely alone this run and
            # report it. (A later run reads the finished marker and decides.)
            skipped["marker"] += 1
            continue
        if mt is not None and la <= 0:
            # A marker exists but the record has no usable timestamp, so the
            # two cannot be compared. Never delete on that; never copy either.
            skipped["timestamp"] += 1
            continue

        # A record is deleted only when a marker REALLY exists and is at least
        # as new as a usable lastActivityAt. "No marker" must never read as 0
        # and match a record whose timestamp is missing.
        dead = do_delete and mt is not None and la > 0 and mt >= la

        for p in paths:
            dst = os.path.join(p, sid + ".json")
            # lexists: a dangling symlink under the record's name is still
            # something on disk. load_record() refuses it below, so it is
            # left alone as an unreadable destination, never replaced.
            exists = os.path.lexists(dst)
            lt = local_dels.get((p, sid), 0)

            if not do_delete and lt > 0:
                # Deletion propagation is off, so this run may not unlink a
                # marker either. Writing the record into a store that holds ANY
                # marker for it -- its own deletion, or one that is merely
                # stale -- would leave record and marker side by side, which is
                # the combination that can hide the session, and no later
                # default run could repair it. Leave this store alone.
                # (Cheap check first: no need to read a destination we are not
                # going to touch.)
                continue

            # Whatever happens next -- a write or a delete -- depends on what
            # this store actually holds, so read it now, and remember exactly
            # which bytes the decision was made about.
            old = {}
            ident = None
            if exists:
                before = stat_ident(dst)
                loaded = load_record(dst)
                if loaded is None:
                    # The destination is being rewritten right now, or is
                    # damaged. Treating it as an empty record would let an
                    # older copy overwrite the newest one and would throw away
                    # the target's own bridgeSessionIds; deleting it would
                    # throw away a file we cannot even read. Skip this store
                    # for this session; the next run sees the finished file.
                    skipped["dest"] += 1
                    if dead:
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                    continue
                ident = stat_ident(dst)
                if ident is None or ident != before:
                    # It changed while it was being read, so `loaded` may be
                    # neither the old nor the new content.
                    skipped["changed"] += 1
                    if dead:
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                    continue
                old = loaded
                if names_other_session(old, sid):
                    # The body names a DIFFERENT session, so this file may be
                    # that session's only copy. scan() never uses such a file
                    # as a source; it is no target either -- not overwritten
                    # by this session's winner, not unlinked for this
                    # session's deletion, and no marker goes next to it.
                    skipped["destsid"] += 1
                    if dead:
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                    continue
                if (old.get("lastActivityAt") is not None
                        and ms(old.get("lastActivityAt")) <= 0
                        and os.path.realpath(src) != os.path.realpath(dst)):
                    # This copy carries a timestamp we cannot read: seconds
                    # instead of milliseconds, a half-written number, garbage.
                    # It is therefore not "older than everything", it is
                    # undatable -- and an undatable destination must not be
                    # overwritten by an older-but-well-formed copy, nor
                    # unlinked. Exactly like an unreadable destination: leave
                    # this store's copy alone and report it.
                    skipped["destclock"] += 1
                    if dead:
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                    continue

            if dead:
                if exists:
                    ola = ms(old.get("lastActivityAt"))
                    if ola <= 0:
                        # No lastActivityAt at all (missing or null), so this
                        # copy cannot be compared with the marker -- and a
                        # record with no usable timestamp is never deleted.
                        # Leave it, and write no marker next to it.
                        skipped["destclock"] += 1
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                        continue
                    if ola > mt:
                        # This copy has been touched since the scan and is now
                        # newer than the deletion marker, so the marker no
                        # longer describes it. Leave it and decide next run.
                        skipped["changed"] += 1
                        held.setdefault(sid, set()).add(p)
                        continue
                    removes.append((dst, best.get("title", "?"), ident, mt))
                continue
            # The record stays in this store. Any older deletion marker next to
            # it is stale (the session was used again after being deleted). The
            # app never produces that combination itself and it can hide the
            # session, so clear it -- but only with --propagate-deletions,
            # which is the only mode that unlinks anything at all.
            if do_delete and 0 < lt < la:
                mk = os.path.join(p, "deleted_" + sid[len("local_"):])
                stale.append((mk, stat_ident(mk)))
            if os.path.realpath(src) == os.path.realpath(dst):
                continue
            if exists and ms(old.get("lastActivityAt")) >= la:
                continue
            writes.append((dst, best, src, old, la, exists, ident))

    # A marker nobody could read, for a session no store holds a usable
    # record for, is still an undecidable item -- and the docs promise every
    # one of them is reported. The loop above only ever sees sessions that
    # are in `recs`, so the rest are counted here.
    for sid in unclear:
        if sid not in recs:
            skipped["marker"] += 1

    if do_delete:
        # Propagate markers -- but only for genuinely dead sessions, and only
        # into a store where the marker cannot end up next to a record.
        # Everything in `dels` parsed cleanly, so no '0' marker is ever created.
        for sid, t in sorted(dels.items()):
            if sid in unclear:
                continue
            rec = recs.get(sid)
            if rec is not None:
                la = ms(rec[0].get("lastActivityAt"))
                if la <= 0 or t < la:
                    # A stale marker for a still-live session. Never copied.
                    continue
            elif sid in on_disk:
                # No usable record, but a file for this session IS on disk
                # somewhere -- unparseable, an unusable name, a body naming a
                # different session, no cliSessionId. A file that could not be
                # interpreted blocks propagation exactly like `unclear` does:
                # the alternative is writing a marker next to a living record.
                skipped["propagate"] += 1
                continue
            kept = held.get(sid) or set()
            for p in paths:
                if p in kept:
                    # The record is still in this store: its removal was
                    # postponed this run, so the marker would hide it here.
                    skipped["propagate"] += 1
                    continue
                if rec is None and os.path.exists(os.path.join(p, sid + ".json")):
                    # It appeared after the scan.
                    skipped["propagate"] += 1
                    continue
                mk = os.path.join(p, "deleted_" + sid[len("local_"):])
                if not os.path.lexists(mk):
                    marks.append((mk, t))
    return writes, removes, marks, stale, skipped


def marker_path_for(record_path):
    """store/local_<id>.json -> store/deleted_<id>"""
    d, b = os.path.split(record_path)
    return os.path.join(d, "deleted_" + b[len("local_"):-len(".json")])


def marker_on_disk(record_path):
    """True when the record's store holds ANY entry under that session's
    marker name right now -- readable, stale, or not a file at all."""
    return os.path.lexists(marker_path_for(record_path))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backups
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def store_key(path):
    """Backup-relative key for a store path. Round-trips via store_from_key().

        main/<accountUuid>/<orgUuid>
        profiles/<profile>/<accountUuid>/<orgUuid>
    """
    if path.startswith(BASE + os.sep):
        rel = os.path.relpath(path, BASE).split(os.sep)
        if len(rel) == 2:
            return os.path.join("main", rel[0], rel[1])
    elif path.startswith(PROFILES_ROOT + os.sep):
        rel = os.path.relpath(path, PROFILES_ROOT).split(os.sep)
        if len(rel) == 4 and rel[1] == "claude-code-sessions":
            return os.path.join("profiles", rel[0], rel[2], rel[3])
    return None


def store_from_key(key):
    """Inverse of store_key(). Returns None for anything unrecognised."""
    parts = key.split(os.sep)
    if len(parts) == 3 and parts[0] == "main":
        return os.path.join(BASE, parts[1], parts[2])
    if len(parts) == 4 and parts[0] == "profiles":
        return os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, parts[1], "claude-code-sessions",
                            parts[2], parts[3])
    return None


def backup_time(path):
    """When a backup was taken, as a unix timestamp.

    Read from the name's own stamp first, so the collision suffix a second run
    in the same second gets ("...-2") cannot reorder anything: both parse to
    the same second and the name then breaks the tie in the right direction.
    Only a name that does not parse falls back to the directory's mtime.
    """
    name = os.path.basename(path)[len(BACKUP_PREFIX):]
    m = re.match(r"^(\d{8}-\d{6})", name)
    if m:
        try:
            return datetime.datetime.strptime(m.group(1), "%Y%m%d-%H%M%S").timestamp()
        except ValueError:
            pass
    try:
        return os.stat(path).st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return 0.0


def backup_dirs():
    """Complete backup directories, oldest first. A `.partial` directory is a
    backup that was interrupted: it is never listed, never restored from and
    never counted as "this machine has been backed up before"."""
    out = []
    for n in scan_names(BACKUP_ROOT):
        if not n.startswith(BACKUP_PREFIX) or n.endswith(PARTIAL_SUFFIX):
            continue
        p = os.path.join(BACKUP_ROOT, n)
        if os.path.isdir(p):
            out.append(p)
    # By time, not lexicographically: the same-second suffix breaks name order
    # as soon as a freed base name is reused, and the newest backup sorting as
    # the oldest means the wrong one is pruned and the wrong one suggested by
    # --restore-list.
    out.sort(key=lambda p: (backup_time(p), os.path.basename(p)))
    return out


def backup_files(store_path):
    """The index files of one store -- records and deletion markers only, or
    None when the store cannot be listed.

    None, never []: "I could not look" must not reach a caller as "there was
    nothing to copy", or a backup that contains none of a store's records
    reports itself as complete coverage of it.
    Only regular files: a symlink is not followed out of the store (and no
    run ever writes over or unlinks one, so there is nothing to back up), and
    a fifo is never opened.
    Conversation transcripts live elsewhere and are never included."""
    names = list_dir(store_path)
    if names is None:
        return None
    out = []
    for name in names:
        if not (is_record_name(name) or name.startswith("deleted_")):
            continue
        f = os.path.join(store_path, name)
        if is_regular_entry(f):
            out.append(f)
    return out


def is_pre_delete(path):
    """True for a backup that was taken before a run that deleted something."""
    return os.path.exists(os.path.join(path, PRE_DELETE_MARK))


def prune_backups(protect=None):
    """Keep the newest KEEP_BACKUPS backups, with two floors on top.

    `protect` is never evicted: a --restore takes a safety backup first, and
    that must not push out the very backup it is restoring from.

    Nothing younger than MIN_BACKUP_AGE is evicted either, and the NEWEST
    KEEP_PRE_DELETE pre-deletion backups, plus the very first one, are kept
    whatever the count says. A count on its own is not retention here: the
    background job re-plans a destructive run it could not complete every
    minute, and KEEP_BACKUPS fresh backups in KEEP_BACKUPS minutes would throw
    away the one copy of the records the first destructive run removed.

    Which end is protected matters. Each pre-deletion backup is the only copy
    of the state before one destructive run (or one --restore, whose safety
    backup is marked the same way), and the run someone wants to undo is
    nearly always a RECENT one: a hidden session is noticed within days.
    Protecting the oldest three instead evicted the most recent destructive
    runs first, and with them the safety backup of every --restore. The very
    first pre-deletion backup is kept as well, because it is the state before
    this tool deleted anything at all. At most KEEP_PRE_DELETE + 1 backups are
    therefore unprunable, so that set cannot grow without bound.
    """
    keep = os.path.realpath(protect) if protect else None
    now = time.time()
    dirs = [d for d in backup_dirs()
            if not (keep and os.path.realpath(d) == keep)]
    pre_delete = [d for d in dirs if is_pre_delete(d)]  # oldest first
    keep_set = set(dirs[-KEEP_BACKUPS:] if KEEP_BACKUPS else dirs)
    if KEEP_PRE_DELETE:
        keep_set.update(pre_delete[-KEEP_PRE_DELETE:])
    keep_set.update(pre_delete[:1])
    # Only when the directory has run away does the age floor give, and then
    # oldest first -- see HARD_MAX_BACKUPS. The protected pre-deletion set is
    # in keep_set, so it is never among them.
    over_budget = len(dirs) - HARD_MAX_BACKUPS
    for d in dirs:  # oldest first
        if d in keep_set:
            continue
        if now - backup_time(d) < MIN_BACKUP_AGE:
            if over_budget <= 0:
                continue
            over_budget -= 1
        try:
            shutil.rmtree(d)
        except OSError:
            pass


def backup_fingerprint(paths):
    """A digest of every index file that a backup of `paths` would contain.

    Name, size and mtime_ns of each file, under its backup-relative key. Two
    runs get the same digest only when nothing in any store has been touched,
    which is what makes an existing backup reusable as this run's safety net.
    None when anything could not be stat'ed or listed -- then nothing may be
    reused, because a store we cannot enumerate could have changed in any way
    at all and the digest would not show it.
    """
    items = []
    for p in sorted(paths):
        key = store_key(p)
        if key is None:
            continue
        files = backup_files(p)
        if files is None:
            return None
        for f in files:
            try:
                st = os.stat(f)
            except OSError:
                return None
            items.append("%s/%s|%d|%d"
                         % (key, os.path.basename(f), st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns))
    return hashlib.sha256("\n".join(sorted(items)).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def read_small(path):
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            return fh.read(512).strip()
    except OSError:
        return None


def backup_covers(bdir, require):
    """True when `bdir` holds a copy of every file the run may destroy.

    `require` maps a store this run deletes from to the index file names it
    held during the scan. A name that is no longer on disk is not required --
    the app may have removed it since -- but anything still there must be in
    the backup, or this backup is not a safety net for this run.

    An empty `expected` now means what it says: the store was listed and held
    no index files. A store whose listing FAILED never reaches this function,
    because sync() drops it from the run before anything is planned for it.
    """
    for p, expected in (require or {}).items():
        if not expected:
            continue
        key = store_key(p)
        if key is None:
            return False
        for name in expected:
            # Same test as backup_files(): only a regular file can be at risk
            # (nothing ever overwrites or unlinks anything else), so only a
            # regular file has to be in the backup.
            if not is_regular_entry(os.path.join(p, name)):
                continue
            if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(bdir, key, name)):
                return False
    return True


def make_backup(paths, reason, protect=None, require=None, pre_delete=False,
                fingerprint=None):
    """Copy every store's index files into a timestamped backup directory.

    Returns the directory, or None if the backup could not be completed. The
    caller MUST treat None as "there is no safety net" and delete nothing.

    "Completed" means more than "no exception". A store whose path cannot be
    expressed as a backup key, or whose directory cannot be listed at all,
    used to be skipped silently while the run went on to delete from it -- an
    unreadable listing looked exactly like an empty store. So every store in
    `require` -- the stores this run is about to delete from -- must really
    have its files in here, a store that could not be enumerated is a failure
    whenever the run would touch it (there is no way to prove a backup covers
    a directory you cannot read), and a backup that copied nothing while
    stores do hold files is a failure too. A hollow directory must never be renamed into
    place: it would count as "this machine has been backed up before", and it
    would occupy a retention slot that a real backup needs.

    A single file that cannot be COPIED (mode 000, gone since the listing) is
    not a failure unless it is one of the files in `require`: it is left out
    and named in a warning. Failing the whole backup over it meant one
    unreadable index file anywhere made every backup fail for good -- the
    first apply then wrote with no backup at all, and every deletion was
    postponed for ever, also in stores that file had nothing to do with.

    The copy is assembled in <name>.partial and renamed into place only once it
    is complete, so a backup that fails half way never appears in
    backup_dirs(), in --restore-list, or as "this machine already has backups".
    """
    stamp = datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    dest_root = os.path.join(BACKUP_ROOT, BACKUP_PREFIX + stamp)
    n = 2
    while os.path.exists(dest_root):  # two runs within the same second
        dest_root = os.path.join(BACKUP_ROOT, "%s%s-%d" % (BACKUP_PREFIX, stamp, n))
        n += 1
    partial = dest_root + PARTIAL_SUFFIX
    if fingerprint is None:
        fingerprint = backup_fingerprint(paths)
    count = 0
    copied = {}      # realpath(store) -> names actually copied
    unmapped = []    # stores holding files that no backup key can describe
    unlisted = []    # stores whose contents could not even be enumerated
    uncopied = []    # (file, error) for every file that could not be copied
    try:
        os.makedirs(BACKUP_ROOT, exist_ok=True)
        chmod_quiet(BACKUP_ROOT, DIR_MODE)
        if os.path.exists(partial):
            shutil.rmtree(partial)
        os.makedirs(partial)
        chmod_quiet(partial, DIR_MODE)
        for p in paths:
            key = store_key(p)
            files = backup_files(p)
            if files is None:
                # Cannot be enumerated, so it cannot be copied and cannot be
                # shown to be covered. sync() drops such a store from the run
                # long before this point; if one turns up here anyway it is a
                # failed backup whenever the run would touch it.
                unlisted.append(p)
                if p in (require or {}):
                    raise RuntimeError(
                        "%s could not be listed, so this backup cannot cover "
                        "it, and this run would delete from it" % p)
                continue
            if key is None:
                if files:
                    unmapped.append(p)
                continue
            if not files:
                continue
            d = os.path.join(partial, key)
            os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
            chmod_quiet(d, DIR_MODE)
            got = copied.setdefault(os.path.realpath(p), set())
            for f in files:
                base = os.path.basename(f)
                try:
                    shutil.copy2(f, os.path.join(d, base))
                except OSError as e:
                    # Left out, not fatal: the coverage check below still
                    # fails this backup if it is a file the run could
                    # destroy. A half-written copy must not stay behind, or
                    # --restore would put it back over the real file.
                    uncopied.append((f, e))
                    if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(d, base)):
                        try:
                            os.unlink(os.path.join(d, base))
                        except OSError:
                            pass
                    continue
                chmod_quiet(os.path.join(d, base), FILE_MODE)
                got.add(base)
                count += 1

        # Does this cover what the run is about to destroy?
        for p, expected in (require or {}).items():
            if not expected:
                continue
            got = copied.get(os.path.realpath(p), set())
            if store_key(p) is None:
                raise RuntimeError(
                    "%s cannot be represented in a backup, and this run would "
                    "delete from it" % p)
            lost = sorted(name for name in expected
                          if name not in got
                          and is_regular_entry(os.path.join(p, name)))
            if lost:
                raise RuntimeError(
                    "%d file(s) in %s could not be backed up (e.g. %s), and "
                    "this run would delete from it" % (len(lost), p, lost[0]))
        if count == 0 and (unmapped or uncopied
                           or any((require or {}).values())):
            raise RuntimeError("nothing could be copied although the stores "
                               "hold index files")

        if pre_delete:
            with open(os.path.join(partial, PRE_DELETE_MARK), "w",
                      encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(reason + "\n")
        if fingerprint:
            with open(os.path.join(partial, FINGERPRINT_FILE), "w",
                      encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(fingerprint + "\n")
        os.rename(partial, dest_root)
        chmod_quiet(dest_root, DIR_MODE)
    except Exception as e:
        log("WARNING backup failed: %s" % e, force=True)
        try:
            shutil.rmtree(partial)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return None
    if unmapped:
        log("WARNING backup does not cover %d store(s) outside the known "
            "layout: %s" % (len(unmapped), unmapped[0]), force=True)
    if unlisted:
        log("WARNING backup does not cover %d store(s) that could not be "
            "listed: %s" % (len(unlisted), unlisted[0]), force=True)
    if uncopied:
        log("WARNING backup left out %d file(s) that could not be copied -- "
            "none of them is one this run would remove: %s (%s)"
            % (len(uncopied), uncopied[0][0], uncopied[0][1]), force=True)
    log("backup: %d file%s -> %s  (%s)"
        % (count, "" if count == 1 else "s", dest_root, reason), force=True)
    prune_backups(protect)
    return dest_root


def ensure_backup(paths, reason, require=None, pre_delete=False):
    """The safety net for this run: an existing identical backup, or a new one.

    A destructive plan the run cannot complete is recomputed every minute by
    the background job, and taking a fresh full backup each time only churns
    the retention window. When the newest backup is recent, was taken over a
    set of index files that still has the same names, sizes and mtimes (see
    backup_fingerprint; contents are not read), and holds everything this run
    could destroy, it IS this run's backup -- reuse it.
    """
    fp = backup_fingerprint(paths)
    if fp:
        dirs = backup_dirs()
        if dirs:
            newest = dirs[-1]
            if (time.time() - backup_time(newest) < BACKUP_REUSE_AGE
                    and read_small(os.path.join(newest, FINGERPRINT_FILE)) == fp
                    and backup_covers(newest, require)):
                if pre_delete and not is_pre_delete(newest):
                    try:
                        with open(os.path.join(newest, PRE_DELETE_MARK), "w",
                                  encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                            fh.write(reason + "\n")
                    except OSError as e:
                        # It cannot be protected from pruning, so do not rely
                        # on it; take a real one instead.
                        log("WARNING could not mark %s as pre-deletion: %s"
                            % (newest, e), force=True)
                        return make_backup(paths, reason, require=require,
                                           pre_delete=pre_delete, fingerprint=fp)
                log("backup: reusing %s -- no index file has changed since it "
                    "was written  (%s)" % (newest, reason), force=True)
                return newest
    return make_backup(paths, reason, require=require, pre_delete=pre_delete,
                       fingerprint=fp)


def restore_list():
    dirs = backup_dirs()
    if not dirs:
        print("no backups yet in %s" % BACKUP_ROOT)
        print("a backup is taken automatically before the first apply and "
              "before any run that deletes something.")
        return 0
    print("backups in %s (newest last):" % BACKUP_ROOT)
    for d in dirs:
        stamp = os.path.basename(d)[len(BACKUP_PREFIX):]
        recs = markers = nstores = 0
        for root, _dirs, files in os.walk(d):
            here = [f for f in files
                    if f.startswith("local_") or f.startswith("deleted_")]
            if here:
                nstores += 1
            recs += len([f for f in here if f.startswith("local_")])
            markers += len([f for f in here if f.startswith("deleted_")])
        print("   %-20s  %3d record%s  %3d marker%s  %d store%s"
              % (stamp, recs, " " if recs == 1 else "s",
                 markers, " " if markers == 1 else "s",
                 nstores, "" if nstores == 1 else "s"))
    print("\nrestore with: --restore %s"
          % os.path.basename(dirs[-1])[len(BACKUP_PREFIX):])
    return 0


def restore(stamp):
    """Copy a backup's files back into the matching stores.

    Nothing is removed except the deletion markers of the very sessions being
    restored. Those markers are what removed the records in the first place, so
    leaving them in place would make the next sync undo the restore within a
    minute. Every other file, in every store, is left exactly as it is.

    The current state is backed up first -- every existing store the restore
    writes into or clears a marker in, including a profile store the sync
    itself currently skips as frozen -- so a restore can itself be rolled back
    with another --restore. If that safety backup cannot be written, or lacks
    a file the restore would copy over or a marker it would clear, the restore
    does not start at all. A store that cannot be listed cannot be backed up,
    so nothing is written into it and no marker in it is cleared. A backup
    key for a profile that no longer exists (`claude-multi remove`) is
    skipped rather than recreated as a bare directory tree.
    """
    stamp = str(stamp).strip()
    name = stamp if stamp.startswith(BACKUP_PREFIX) else BACKUP_PREFIX + stamp
    if name.endswith(PARTIAL_SUFFIX):
        print("that is an interrupted backup, not a complete one: %s" % name)
        return 1
    src_root = os.path.join(BACKUP_ROOT, name)
    if not os.path.isdir(src_root):
        print("no such backup: %s" % src_root)
        print("run --restore-list to see what is available.")
        return 1

    # Pass 1: read the backup. Group the files by destination store and collect
    # the session ids it holds a record for.
    todo, revive, skipped = [], set(), 0
    for root, _dirs, files in os.walk(src_root):
        files = sorted(f for f in files
                       if f.startswith("local_") or f.startswith("deleted_"))
        if not files:
            continue
        key = os.path.relpath(root, src_root)
        dest = store_from_key(key)
        if dest is None:
            print("   skipped (unrecognised path in backup): %s" % key)
            skipped += len(files)
            continue
        if (dest.startswith(PROFILES_ROOT + os.sep) and not os.path.isdir(
                os.path.join(PROFILES_ROOT, key.split(os.sep)[1]))):
            # The profile was removed. Writing into it would recreate a bare
            # directory tree the sync then skips (no config.json), and its
            # sessions are not revived from here, so no marker elsewhere is
            # cleared on their account either.
            print("   skipped (that profile no longer exists): %s" % key)
            skipped += len(files)
            continue
        todo.append((dest, root, files))
        for f in files:
            if f.startswith("local_") and f.endswith(".json"):
                revive.add(f[:-len(".json")])
    if not todo:
        print("nothing in this backup matches a store on this machine:")
        print("   %s" % src_root)
        return 1

    # A store whose directory cannot be listed cannot be copied into the
    # safety backup, so a restore into it could not be undone -- and a restore
    # overwrites records and clears markers. Leave it completely alone, the
    # same way a sync run drops it.
    live_stores, blind = [], []
    for p in stores():
        (blind if list_dir(p) is None else live_stores).append(p)
    # The safety backup has to cover every store this restore can write into
    # or clear a marker in -- not only the ones the sync syncs today. A backup
    # key can name a profile store the sync now treats as frozen (idle and
    # unregistered, say), and the restore writes into that one as well. One
    # that cannot be listed cannot be backed up, so it is left alone too.
    touched = list(live_stores)
    for d, _r, _f in todo:
        if d in touched or d in blind or not os.path.isdir(d):
            continue
        (blind if list_dir(d) is None else touched).append(d)
    blind_real = set(os.path.realpath(p) for p in blind)
    for p in blind:
        print("   skipped (cannot be listed, so it cannot be backed up): %s" % p)

    # A restore is a rollback: every backed-up record is copied over the
    # current one without comparing timestamps. That is what it is for, and
    # the safety backup below makes it reversible -- but a record that is
    # NEWER on disk than in the backup goes back in time, and the user has to
    # be told that, with numbers, before anything is touched.
    replaced, rollback = 0, []
    for dest, root, files in todo:
        if os.path.realpath(dest) in blind_real:
            continue
        for f in files:
            if not (f.startswith("local_") and f.endswith(".json")):
                continue
            cur_path = os.path.join(dest, f)
            if not is_regular_entry(cur_path):
                continue
            replaced += 1
            cur = load_record(cur_path)
            old = load_record(os.path.join(root, f))
            if cur is None or old is None:
                continue
            if ms(cur.get("lastActivityAt")) > ms(old.get("lastActivityAt")):
                rollback.append((str(cur.get("title", "?")), label(dest)))
    if replaced:
        n = len(rollback)
        print("this restore replaces %d record%s that exist%s now; %s"
              % (replaced, "" if replaced == 1 else "s",
                 "s" if replaced == 1 else "",
                 ("it is not newer on disk than in the backup." if replaced == 1
                  else "none of them is newer on disk than in the backup.")
                 if not n else
                 "%d of them %s newer on disk than in the backup and %s rolled "
                 "back to the backup's older copy:"
                 % (n, "is" if n == 1 else "are", "is" if n == 1 else "are")))
        for title, where in rollback[:5]:
            print("   ROLLBACK  %-46s  %s" % (title[:44], where))
        if n > 5:
            print("   ... and %d more" % (n - 5))
        if n:
            print("   (a store this backup does not cover that holds the newer "
                  "copy hands it back\n    on the next sync -- newest wins.)")
        print("the current state is backed up first, so this restore can itself "
              "be undone with\nanother --restore.\n")

    # A restore overwrites current records, so make it undoable -- and refuse
    # to run if it cannot be. `protect` keeps this backup from being pruned by
    # the safety backup that is about to be taken.
    # pre_delete: this one holds the only copy of the state the restore is
    # about to overwrite, and it clears markers too, so retention must protect
    # it the same way it protects a backup taken before a deletion.
    # `require`: every file the restore can destroy -- each existing file it
    # copies over, each marker it clears -- must really be in that backup,
    # or the restore does not start.
    clears = set("deleted_" + s[len("local_"):] for s in revive)
    require = {}
    for d in touched:
        here = set(list_dir(d) or [])
        at_risk = here & clears
        for dest, _root, files in todo:
            if dest == d:
                at_risk.update(here.intersection(files))
        require[d] = at_risk
    if make_backup(touched, "before --restore " + name[len(BACKUP_PREFIX):],
                   protect=src_root, require=require, pre_delete=True) is None:
        print("ERROR the current state could not be backed up, so nothing was "
              "restored.")
        print("free some space or fix the permissions on %s, then try again."
              % BACKUP_ROOT)
        return 1

    restored = 0
    beside_live, not_file = [], []
    for dest, root, files in todo:
        if (os.path.realpath(dest) in blind_real
                or (os.path.isdir(dest) and list_dir(dest) is None)):
            print("   ERROR %s cannot be listed, so it is not in the safety "
                  "backup; nothing was restored into it" % dest)
            continue
        try:
            os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)
        except OSError as e:
            print("   ERROR could not create %s: %s" % (dest, e))
            continue
        wrote = 0
        for f in files:
            target = os.path.join(dest, f)
            if f.startswith("deleted_"):
                sid = "local_" + f[len("deleted_"):]
                if sid in revive:
                    # This marker is what deleted a record the backup is
                    # bringing back. Writing it again would cancel the restore.
                    continue
                if os.path.lexists(os.path.join(dest, sid + ".json")):
                    # The store holds a live record for that session NOW (it
                    # was used again after the backup was taken). A marker
                    # next to it is the pair that hides a session -- a state
                    # the backup never contained -- so it is left out.
                    beside_live.append(target)
                    continue
            try:
                st = os.lstat(target)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                st = None
            except OSError as e:
                print("   ERROR restoring %s: %s" % (f, e))
                continue
            if st is not None and not stat_mod.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
                # A directory, a symlink or a fifo under an index file's name.
                # copy2 would write INTO it or THROUGH it; never do either.
                not_file.append(target)
                continue
            try:
                shutil.copy2(os.path.join(root, f), target)
                wrote += 1
            except Exception as e:
                print("   ERROR restoring %s: %s" % (f, e))
        restored += wrote
        print("   %-24s <- %d file%s" % (label(dest), wrote,
                                         "" if wrote == 1 else "s"))

    # Clear the markers for the restored sessions in EVERY store, not only the
    # ones this backup covers: the deletion timestamp used by the sync is the
    # maximum across all stores, so a single surviving marker anywhere would
    # delete the session again on the next run.
    targets = set(live_stores)
    targets.update(d for d, _r, _f in todo
                   if os.path.realpath(d) not in blind_real)
    cleared = []
    for store in sorted(targets):
        for sid in sorted(revive):
            mk = os.path.join(store, "deleted_" + sid[len("local_"):])
            if not os.path.lexists(mk):
                continue
            if not is_regular_entry(mk):
                # Not a marker file: a directory, a symlink, a fifo. Only a
                # marker is cleared. The sync leaves that session alone while
                # the entry is there, which is reported below.
                not_file.append(mk)
                continue
            try:
                os.unlink(mk)
                cleared.append(mk)
            except OSError as e:
                print("   ERROR could not clear %s: %s" % (mk, e))

    print("\nrestored %d file%s from %s"
          % (restored, "" if restored == 1 else "s", src_root))
    if cleared:
        print("cleared %d deletion marker%s belonging to the restored sessions "
              "(otherwise the\nnext sync would delete them again):"
              % (len(cleared), "" if len(cleared) == 1 else "s"))
        for mk in cleared[:5]:
            print("   %s" % mk)
        if len(cleared) > 5:
            print("   ... and %d more" % (len(cleared) - 5))
    if beside_live:
        print("left out %d deletion marker%s from the backup: that store now "
              "holds a live record\nfor the session, and a marker next to it "
              "would hide it:"
              % (len(beside_live), "" if len(beside_live) == 1 else "s"))
        for mk in beside_live[:5]:
            print("   %s" % mk)
        if len(beside_live) > 5:
            print("   ... and %d more" % (len(beside_live) - 5))
    if not_file:
        print("left alone %d entr%s that %s not a regular file (a directory, a "
              "symlink, a fifo)\nunder an index file's name. Nothing was "
              "written into or through %s, and the sync\nwill not touch %s "
              "either -- remove %s by hand:"
              % (len(not_file), "y" if len(not_file) == 1 else "ies",
                 "is" if len(not_file) == 1 else "are",
                 "it" if len(not_file) == 1 else "them",
                 "it" if len(not_file) == 1 else "them",
                 "it" if len(not_file) == 1 else "them"))
        for p in not_file[:5]:
            print("   %s" % p)
        if len(not_file) > 5:
            print("   ... and %d more" % (len(not_file) - 5))
    if skipped:
        print("skipped %d file%s in unrecognised locations or removed profiles"
              % (skipped, "" if skipped == 1 else "s"))
    print("nothing else was removed; anything this backup does not know about "
          "was left alone.")
    print("\nrestart Claude Desktop (or switch accounts) to see the restored list.")
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# applying
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def sweep_stray_temps(paths):
    """Delete leftover temp files from a run that was killed between open() and
    os.replace(). They are this tool's own scratch files -- never a record and
    never a marker -- so this runs in the default mode too, which removes
    nothing else at all.

    Only the exact name the writer produces is swept (TMP_RE). An earlier
    version also swept any `local_*.tmp`, i.e. files this tool never wrote;
    whatever else sits in a store is not ours to remove."""
    now = time.time()
    n = 0
    for p in paths:
        for name in scan_names(p):
            if not TMP_RE.match(name):
                continue
            f = os.path.join(p, name)
            try:
                if not is_regular_entry(f):
                    continue
                if now - os.path.getmtime(f) < STRAY_TMP_AGE:
                    continue
                os.unlink(f)
                n += 1
            except OSError:
                continue
    if n:
        log("cleaned %d stray temp file%s left behind by an interrupted run"
            % (n, "" if n == 1 else "s"), force=True)


def skipped_note(skipped):
    """Human wording for the items plan() deliberately left alone."""
    parts = []
    if skipped.get("marker"):
        parts.append("%d session(s) with an unreadable deletion marker"
                     % skipped["marker"])
    if skipped.get("markertype"):
        n = skipped["markertype"]
        parts.append("%d deletion-marker entr%s that %s not a regular file (a "
                     "directory, a symlink, a fifo): unknown-but-present, so "
                     "that session is left alone in every store until the "
                     "entry is removed by hand"
                     % (n, "y" if n == 1 else "ies", "is" if n == 1 else "are"))
    if skipped.get("pair"):
        parts.append("%d record write(s) not made because the destination "
                     "store holds a deletion marker for that session (a record "
                     "next to a marker can hide it)" % skipped["pair"])
    if skipped.get("timestamp"):
        parts.append("%d session(s) with no usable lastActivityAt next to a marker"
                     % skipped["timestamp"])
    if skipped.get("dest"):
        parts.append("%d store copy(s) that could not be read" % skipped["dest"])
    if skipped.get("destclock"):
        parts.append("%d store copy(s) whose own lastActivityAt is not a "
                     "plausible millisecond timestamp (or, next to a deletion, "
                     "is missing), so they were neither overwritten nor removed"
                     % skipped["destclock"])
    if skipped.get("destsid"):
        parts.append("%d store copy(s) whose sessionId names a different "
                     "session than the file name, so they were neither "
                     "overwritten nor removed" % skipped["destsid"])
    if skipped.get("changed"):
        parts.append("%d store copy(s) that changed while the run was deciding"
                     % skipped["changed"])
    if skipped.get("sid"):
        parts.append("%d record file(s) whose sessionId contradicts the file "
                     "name" % skipped["sid"])
    if skipped.get("name"):
        parts.append("%d record file(s) with an unusable file name"
                     % skipped["name"])
    if skipped.get("clock"):
        parts.append("%d record copy(s) whose lastActivityAt is not a "
                     "plausible millisecond timestamp" % skipped["clock"])
    if skipped.get("unreadable"):
        parts.append("%d record file(s) that could not be read this run"
                     % skipped["unreadable"])
    if skipped.get("nocli"):
        parts.append("%d record file(s) with no cliSessionId (nothing for "
                     "another account to resume)" % skipped["nocli"])
    if skipped.get("propagate"):
        parts.append("%d deletion marker(s) not propagated because a file for "
                     "that session is still on disk" % skipped["propagate"])
    if skipped.get("store"):
        parts.append("%d store(s) dropped from the run: the directory could "
                     "not be listed" % skipped["store"])
    return parts


def sync(apply_, do_delete):
    paths = stores()
    if len(paths) < 2:
        if not apply_:
            print("found %d session store%s, so there is nothing to sync yet:"
                  % (len(paths), "" if len(paths) == 1 else "s"))
            for p in paths:
                print("   %s" % p)
            print("\nA profile that has never been signed in has no store yet. "
                  "Sign in to\nthe second account (see `claude-multi setup`) and "
                  "run this again.")
            return 0
        log("nothing to sync: fewer than two stores found")
        return 0
    recs, dels, local_dels, unclear, seen, sskip, bad = scan(paths)
    if bad:
        # A store that could not be LISTED is not an empty store. It is
        # dropped from the run completely -- not read, not planned for, not
        # written to, not deleted from -- because os.path.exists() and open()
        # would keep working on it while a backup could only copy what it can
        # enumerate, i.e. none of it.
        for p in bad:
            log("WARNING %s could not be listed, so the whole store is "
                "dropped from this run: nothing is read from it, written to "
                "it or removed from it, and its deletion markers are ignored"
                % p, force=True)
        dropped = set(bad)
        paths = [p for p in paths if p not in dropped]
        if len(paths) < 2:
            msg = ("fewer than two readable stores left after dropping %d "
                   "unreadable one(s) -- nothing to sync" % len(bad))
            if apply_:
                log(msg, force=True)
            else:
                print(msg)
            return 0
    writes, removes, marks, stale, skipped = plan(
        paths, recs, dels, local_dels, unclear, seen, do_delete)
    for k, v in sskip.items():
        skipped[k] = skipped.get(k, 0) + v
    if bad:
        skipped["store"] = skipped.get("store", 0) + len(bad)
    # What this run could destroy, store by store: the records it would unlink
    # and the stale markers it would clear, by name. A backup that does not
    # contain those is not a safety net for this run. Any OTHER file the
    # backup cannot copy is left out with a warning (see make_backup): one
    # unreadable file must not postpone every deletion in every store.
    require = {}
    for dst, _title, _ident, _mt in removes:
        require.setdefault(os.path.dirname(dst), set()).add(os.path.basename(dst))
    for mk, _ident in stale:
        require.setdefault(os.path.dirname(mk), set()).add(os.path.basename(mk))
    for mk, _t in marks:
        # Writing a marker hides the session in that store just as surely as
        # unlinking the record does, and --restore undoes it by clearing the
        # marker again -- so that store has to be in the backup. Nothing on
        # disk there is overwritten (a marker is only ever created, next to no
        # record), so no file in it is required.
        require.setdefault(os.path.dirname(mk), set())
    act = active_store()
    destructive = bool(removes or marks or stale)
    first_apply = not backup_dirs()

    if not apply_:
        print("deletion propagation: %s"
              % ("ON (--propagate-deletions): a session whose marker is at "
                 "least as new as\n                      the winning record "
                 "is removed from every store and the marker\n"
                 "                      is copied there"
                 if do_delete else
                 "OFF (the default): no record or marker is ever unlinked.\n"
                 "                      Records are only added, or replaced "
                 "by a strictly newer copy;\n"
                 "                      a store that holds a marker for a "
                 "session is left alone.\n"
                 "                      Pass --propagate-deletions to apply "
                 "deletions as well."))
        print("stores (%d):" % len(paths))
        for p in paths:
            flag = ""
            if same_dir(p, act):
                flag = "  <- the store the app is using now"
            print("   %3d  %-22s%s" % (record_count(p), label(p), flag))
        print("\nunique sessions       : %d" % len(recs))
        print("records to write      : %d" % len(writes))
        for dst, best, src, old, la, exists, ident in sorted(
                writes, key=lambda x: -x[4])[:15]:
            print("   %s  %-46s -> %s"
                  % ("update" if exists else "NEW   ",
                     str(best.get("title", "?"))[:44], label(os.path.dirname(dst))))
        suffix = "" if do_delete else "   (deletion propagation is off)"
        print("records to remove (deleted)  : %d%s" % (len(removes), suffix))
        for dst, title, _ident, _mt in removes[:10]:
            print("   DELETE  %-46s -> %s" % (str(title)[:44], label(os.path.dirname(dst))))
        print("deletion markers to propagate: %d%s" % (len(marks), suffix))
        print("stale markers to clear       : %d%s" % (len(stale), suffix))
        notes = skipped_note(skipped)
        if notes:
            print("\nleft untouched this run (something could not be read):")
            for n in notes:
                print("   %s" % n)
            print("   nothing is deleted or copied on a guess; a later run "
                  "decides once the\n   file is complete.")
        if (writes or destructive) and (destructive or first_apply):
            print("\na backup would be written to %s/%s<timestamp>/"
                  % (BACKUP_ROOT, BACKUP_PREFIX))
        print("\nrun with --apply to make these changes.")
        print("note: the lines above contain your own session titles and "
              "profile names --\nredact them before pasting this into a bug "
              "report.")
        return 0

    sweep_stray_temps(paths)

    if (writes or destructive) and (destructive or first_apply):
        reason = ("before deleting records" if destructive
                  else "first apply on this machine")
        if ensure_backup(paths, reason, require=require,
                         pre_delete=destructive) is None:
            # No safety net. Nothing may be deleted: not a record, not a
            # marker, not a stale marker. Writes still go ahead -- they only
            # ever replace a record with a strictly newer copy that remains on
            # disk in the store it came from, so they are recoverable without
            # a backup; a deletion is not. (That holds because plan() refuses
            # to overwrite a destination it could not read, could not date, or
            # whose body names another session: "strictly newer" is a
            # comparison between two timestamps we actually understood, about
            # one session, never a default win over an unreadable one.)
            if destructive:
                # The stale markers stay where they are this run, so every
                # write planned into a store that still holds a marker for
                # that session has to stay out as well: written, it would
                # leave record and marker side by side -- the pair that can
                # hide the session, created by this tool, and one no later
                # default run could repair. The same rule the default mode
                # applies in plan().
                held_back = [w for w in writes if marker_on_disk(w[0])]
                writes = [w for w in writes if not marker_on_disk(w[0])]
                skipped["pair"] = skipped.get("pair", 0) + len(held_back)
                log("ERROR backup failed, so nothing is deleted this run: "
                    "%d removal(s), %d marker(s) and %d stale marker(s) "
                    "postponed until a backup can be written%s"
                    % (len(removes), len(marks), len(stale),
                       ", and with them %d record write(s) into a store that "
                       "still holds a marker for that session"
                       % len(held_back) if held_back else ""), force=True)
            removes, marks, stale = [], [], []
            destructive = False

    wrote = removed = marked = cleared = 0
    # Everything below re-checks its target immediately before touching it.
    # The plan was made before the sweep and the backup, which on a large store
    # is seconds to minutes ago, and the app writes to the store it has open
    # continuously -- so "the file I decided about" and "the file that is there
    # now" have to be shown to be the same file.
    #
    # ORDER: stale markers are cleared BEFORE any record is written. Written
    # the other way round, a run killed between the two loops -- or a stale
    # marker whose clearing is refused below -- leaves record and marker side
    # by side. This way the worst a crash can leave is a store with neither,
    # which the next run simply fills. (`stale` is always empty in the default
    # mode, which never unlinks a marker.)
    for mk, ident in stale:
        now_ident = stat_ident(mk)
        if now_ident is None:
            continue
        if now_ident != ident:
            # The marker was rewritten after the plan: it may now be newer
            # than the record, in which case clearing it would resurrect a
            # session the user just deleted.
            skipped["changed"] += 1
            continue
        try:
            os.unlink(mk)
            cleared += 1
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        except OSError as e:
            log("ERROR could not clear %s: %s" % (mk, e), force=True)
    for dst, best, src, old, la, exists, ident in writes:
        if stat_ident(dst) != ident:
            # It was created, replaced or removed since the plan. The
            # comparison that chose this winner no longer describes it, and
            # os.replace() would silently discard whatever is there now.
            skipped["changed"] += 1
            continue
        if marker_on_disk(dst):
            # A marker for this session is in this store RIGHT NOW: a stale
            # one whose clearing above was refused or failed, or one the app
            # wrote after the plan (the session was deleted here meanwhile).
            # Never write a record next to it, in either mode.
            skipped["pair"] = skipped.get("pair", 0) + 1
            continue
        if exists:
            # The identity says "same file", not "same bytes". Read it once
            # more: it must still be a record of THIS session, and still
            # strictly older than the copy about to replace it.
            cur = load_record(dst)
            if (cur is None
                    or names_other_session(cur, os.path.basename(dst)[:-len(".json")])
                    or ms(cur.get("lastActivityAt")) >= la
                    or (cur.get("lastActivityAt") is not None
                        and ms(cur.get("lastActivityAt")) <= 0)
                    or stat_ident(dst) != ident):
                skipped["changed"] += 1
                continue
        rec = dict(best)
        # bridgeSessionIds is Remote Control identity and belongs to the source
        # account's cloud. Drop it; keep the target's own value if it has one.
        rec.pop("bridgeSessionIds", None)
        if isinstance(old, dict) and old.get("bridgeSessionIds"):
            rec["bridgeSessionIds"] = old["bridgeSessionIds"]
        # Unique, hidden, and not ending in .json: two overlapping runs cannot
        # share it, and an orphan cannot be mistaken for a record by the app.
        tmp = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(dst), "%s%d-%s%s"
                           % (TMP_PREFIX, os.getpid(), os.path.basename(dst),
                              TMP_SUFFIX))
        try:
            # UTF-8 whatever the locale: under a Latin-1 locale the default
            # encoding wrote bytes nothing can read back as JSON.
            # allow_nan=False: never emit a bare NaN / Infinity, which is not
            # JSON. load_record() already refuses a record holding one; this
            # is the backstop -- the write fails and is reported, instead of
            # leaving a file the app cannot parse.
            with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                json.dump(rec, fh, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False)
                fh.flush()
                os.fsync(fh.fileno())
            try:
                shutil.copystat(src, tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass  # cosmetic only; never lose the write over it
            # Last look, with the temp file complete: writing, fsync and
            # copystat take milliseconds, and the app may have written a newer
            # copy, or deleted the session here, in the meantime. This does
            # not close the window -- nothing short of the app's cooperation
            # can -- but it shrinks it to these two calls and the rename.
            now_ident = stat_ident(dst)
            if now_ident != ident or marker_on_disk(dst):
                k = "changed" if now_ident != ident else "pair"
                skipped[k] = skipped.get(k, 0) + 1
                try:
                    os.unlink(tmp)
                except OSError:
                    pass
                continue
            os.replace(tmp, dst)
            wrote += 1
        except Exception as e:
            log("ERROR could not write %s: %s" % (dst, e), force=True)
            try:
                os.unlink(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass
    for dst, title, ident, mt in removes:
        now_ident = stat_ident(dst)
        if now_ident is None:
            continue  # already gone; nothing to do and nothing to report
        if now_ident != ident:
            # Rewritten since the plan. If the app touched it, the session was
            # resumed after the deletion and must survive; decide next run.
            skipped["changed"] += 1
            continue
        cur = load_record(dst)
        if cur is None:
            skipped["dest"] += 1
            continue
        cla = ms(cur.get("lastActivityAt"))
        if (cla <= 0 or cla > mt
                or names_other_session(cur, os.path.basename(dst)[:-len(".json")])):
            # Newer than the marker, undatable, or now naming a different
            # session: not what the plan decided to remove. Leave it.
            skipped["changed"] += 1
            continue
        try:
            os.unlink(dst)
            removed += 1
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        except OSError as e:
            log("ERROR could not remove %s: %s" % (dst, e), force=True)
    for mk, t in marks:
        rec_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(mk),
                                "local_" + os.path.basename(mk)[len("deleted_"):]
                                + ".json")
        if os.path.lexists(rec_path):
            # A record for this session is in this store RIGHT NOW: its
            # removal above was postponed or failed, or the app re-created it
            # while this run was working. A marker next to a record is the
            # pair that hides a session, and no later run clears it, so the
            # marker is simply not written. Re-checked here and not only in
            # plan(), because the removals happened in between.
            skipped["propagate"] += 1
            continue
        # O_EXCL: `marks` only holds markers that did not exist at plan time.
        # If one exists now it is the store's own, with its own timestamp, and
        # overwriting it would replace the app's decision with ours.
        try:
            fd = os.open(mk, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, FILE_MODE)
        except FileExistsError:
            skipped["changed"] += 1
            continue
        except OSError as e:
            log("ERROR could not write %s: %s" % (mk, e), force=True)
            continue
        try:
            with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(str(t))
            marked += 1
        except Exception as e:
            log("ERROR could not write %s: %s" % (mk, e), force=True)

    # `marked` counts too: a run whose only effect is propagating deletion
    # markers changed the stores and must not be silent. What counts is what
    # actually HAPPENED: a write that is planned but refused every time (a
    # marker whose name differs from the record's only in letter case, which
    # APFS still finds) must not make the background job log a
    # "synced: 0 record(s) written" line every minute. An interactive run
    # still reports what it planned.
    if wrote or removed or marked or cleared or (
            not QUIET and (writes or removes or marks or stale)):
        extra = "" if do_delete else "  [deletion propagation off (default)]"
        if cleared:
            extra += "  [%d stale marker(s) cleared]" % cleared
        notes = skipped_note(skipped)
        if notes:
            extra += "  [left untouched: %s]" % "; ".join(notes)
        # These are the counts that actually succeeded, not the planned ones.
        log("synced: %d record(s) written, %d deleted record(s) removed, "
            "%d marker(s) propagated (%d session(s) / %d store(s))%s"
            % (wrote, removed, marked, len(recs), len(paths), extra),
            force=True)
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# background job
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def python_for_launchd():
    """The interpreter to bake into the plist.

    launchd keeps running this for months, so the path has to be one that will
    still be there. Prefer the system python3: this script is stdlib-only, and
    /usr/bin/python3 outlives virtualenvs, Homebrew upgrades and Xcode being
    moved or removed -- sys.executable is often something like
    /Applications/Xcode.app/.../python3, which would break the job the moment
    Xcode changes. Fall back to the running interpreter only when the system
    one cannot actually run (Command Line Tools not installed).
    """
    system = "/usr/bin/python3"
    if os.path.exists(system):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([system, "-c", "import sys"],
                               capture_output=True, timeout=20)
            if r.returncode == 0:
                return system
        except Exception:
            pass
    exe = sys.executable
    if exe and os.path.isabs(exe) and os.path.exists(exe):
        print("note: %s is not usable, the job will run %s instead." % (system, exe))
        return exe
    return system


def install(interval, do_delete):
    if interval < MIN_INTERVAL:
        print("warning: %ds is too short, launchd throttles below %ds. Using %ds."
              % (interval, MIN_INTERVAL, MIN_INTERVAL))
        interval = MIN_INTERVAL
    args = [python_for_launchd(), SCRIPT, "--apply", "--quiet"]
    if do_delete:
        args.append("--propagate-deletions")
    data = {
        "Label": LABEL,
        "ProgramArguments": args,
        "StartInterval": interval,
        # Also fire on account switch: the app rewrites config.json then.
        "WatchPaths": [os.path.join(APP, "config.json")],
        "RunAtLoad": True,
        # NOT "Background" + LowPriorityIO. Under real load (a VM, a browser
        # and several Claude instances at once) macOS throttles a Background
        # job's disk I/O almost to zero: measured, a single run that takes
        # ~4s in the foreground ran for minutes, and no run completed for
        # ~50 minutes - so nothing synced at all. The job is tiny (a few
        # hundred small files a minute), so standard priority costs nothing.
        "ProcessType": "Standard",
        "LowPriorityIO": False,
        # A file of its own: launchd holds this open and appends to it, so it
        # must not be the log this script rewrites when it trims.
        "StandardErrorPath": ERRLOG,
    }
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(PLIST), exist_ok=True)
    os.makedirs(LOGDIR, exist_ok=True)
    chmod_quiet(LOGDIR, DIR_MODE)
    with open(PLIST, "wb") as fh:
        plistlib.dump(data, fh)
    uid = str(os.getuid())
    subprocess.run(["launchctl", "bootout", "gui/%s/%s" % (uid, LABEL)],
                   capture_output=True)
    r = subprocess.run(["launchctl", "bootstrap", "gui/%s" % uid, PLIST],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        print("launchctl bootstrap failed:", r.stderr.strip())
        return 1
    print("installed: %s" % PLIST)
    print("interval : every %s (and on every account switch)" % human(interval))
    print("deletion : %s" % ("propagated (--propagate-deletions)" if do_delete
                             else "NOT propagated (the default)"))
    print("log      : %s" % LOG)
    print("errors   : %s" % ERRLOG)
    return 0


def uninstall():
    uid = str(os.getuid())
    subprocess.run(["launchctl", "bootout", "gui/%s/%s" % (uid, LABEL)],
                   capture_output=True)
    if os.path.exists(PLIST):
        os.unlink(PLIST)
    print("removed.")
    return 0


def status():
    if os.path.exists(PLIST):
        try:
            with open(PLIST, "rb") as fh:
                d = plistlib.load(fh)
            iv = d.get("StartInterval", 0)
            # --no-delete may still be in a plist installed by an older
            # version. It is a no-op now, and not propagating is the default,
            # so only the opt-in flag is worth looking for.
            prop = "--propagate-deletions" in (d.get("ProgramArguments") or [])
            print("interval : every %s" % human(iv))
            print("deletion : %s" % ("propagated (--propagate-deletions)" if prop
                                     else "NOT propagated (the default)"))
        except Exception as e:
            print("could not read plist:", e)
    else:
        print("background job is not installed. install it with: "
              "--install [--every 1m] [--propagate-deletions]")
    uid = str(os.getuid())
    r = subprocess.run(["launchctl", "print", "gui/%s/%s" % (uid, LABEL)],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
            if re.search(r"\b(runs|last exit code) ", line):
                print("  ", line.strip())
    dirs = backup_dirs()
    if dirs:
        print("backups  : %d kept, newest %s" % (len(dirs), os.path.basename(dirs[-1])))
    try:
        errsize = os.path.getsize(ERRLOG)
    except OSError:
        errsize = 0
    if errsize:
        print("errors   : %s (%d bytes -- the job wrote to stderr)"
              % (ERRLOG, errsize))
    if os.path.exists(LOG):
        print("\nrecent log lines (%s):" % LOG)
        try:
            with open(LOG, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                for line in fh.readlines()[-8:]:
                    print("  ", line.rstrip())
        except OSError as e:
            print("   could not read the log:", e)
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

EPILOG = """\
what is synced:  only the small per-account session index files
                 (~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude-code-sessions/
                  <accountUuid>/<orgUuid>/local_<id>.json)
never touched:   conversation transcripts in ~/.claude/projects/ -- they are
                 not account-scoped and every account already shares them
deletion mode:   OPT-IN, and OFF unless you pass --propagate-deletions.
                 The default run never unlinks a record, never writes or
                 copies a deletion marker and never clears a stale one: it
                 only adds a record to a store that lacks it, or replaces a
                 strictly older copy. It still honours each store's own
                 marker -- a session you deleted in one account is not pushed
                 back into it -- and, since it may not clear a marker either,
                 it writes nothing at all into a store that holds any marker
                 for that session, so it can never leave a record and a
                 marker side by side. The one file an applying run does
                 remove is its own temp file (a leftover from a killed run,
                 or one it decided at the last moment not to use), named
                 exactly .sync-<pid>-local_<id>.json.tmp -- nothing
                 else, whatever its name. (--no-delete is still accepted as a
                 deprecated no-op: it now describes the default.)
--propagate-deletions:
                 turns the deletion path on. A session is then gone when a
                 deleted_<uuid> marker really exists and is at least as new
                 as the winning record: the record is removed from every
                 store and the marker is copied there. An older marker is
                 stale and gets cleared instead. A marker is only ever copied
                 into other stores when NO store holds a file for that
                 session -- a file that exists but could not be interpreted
                 blocks it, because a marker written next to a live record
                 hides the session for good. Stale markers are cleared
                 before any record is written, and if the backup fails the
                 whole destructive part is cancelled together with every
                 write into a store that still holds a marker for that
                 session, so this mode never creates that pair either
unreadable file: a record or marker caught half-written is skipped for that run
                 rather than guessed at, so a damaged file neither deletes a
                 session nor brings a deleted one back; the dry run lists them.
                 A record holding a number that is not finite (NaN, Infinity,
                 or a literal like 1e999 that overflows) counts as unreadable
                 too: it is not copied, and no such token is ever written.
                 A record whose sessionId field contradicts its own file name,
                 whose lastActivityAt is not a plausible millisecond value
                 (before 2001, or more than a day ahead of this machine's
                 clock), or that has no cliSessionId (nothing for another
                 account to resume) is skipped and reported the same way. A
                 destination copy that cannot be read, cannot be dated, or
                 whose sessionId names a different session is never
                 overwritten or removed. A deleted_<uuid> entry that is not a
                 regular file (a directory, a symlink, a fifo) is a marker
                 that cannot be read: that session is left alone everywhere
                 and reported, and the entry is never touched. A store
                 DIRECTORY that cannot be listed is not an empty store: the
                 whole store is dropped from the run
profiles:        an extra profile under ~/.claude-profiles counts as a live
                 store only when its name is not a parked copy, it really is
                 an app profile directory (it has a config.json), and it is
                 either registered in accounts.tsv (`claude-multi list`
                 refreshes that from e-mail-named, signed-in profiles) or the
                 app itself has written one of its own files there in the
                 last 30 days -- this tool's writes into the session store do
                 not count. Anything else is a snapshot: not read, not
                 written, and its markers are ignored. The run names it once,
                 with the remedy
main app:        a store that holds records or markers is found by listing.
                 A main app freshly signed in to an account has an EMPTY
                 store, which is found through two files that are only read:
                 the app's own config.json (which account it is signed in to)
                 and the last 256 KB of its main.log (that account's newest
                 initialization line, which names the org). That store is
                 adopted only when the line is at least 10 seconds old by its
                 own time stamp and no later line for another account names
                 the same org -- during an account switch the app briefly
                 logs a pair it does not keep. Otherwise nothing is guessed,
                 and a later run decides
--wait SECONDS:  applies to every run that takes the lock (the dry run,
                 --apply, --restore): wait that long for a run already in
                 progress instead of exiting at once. Capped at 300; ignored
                 by --status, --install, --uninstall and --restore-list
backups:         taken automatically before anything is deleted and before the
                 first apply, into ~/.claude/backups/; only the index files,
                 never a transcript. A backup that does not contain every file
                 the run could destroy counts as failed, and nothing is deleted
                 on a run without a backup; any other file it cannot copy is
                 left out with a warning. Newest 7 kept, and one younger than
                 24h is kept too - up to a hard cap of 28, above which the
                 oldest go even if younger. Never pruned, cap or not: the 3
                 NEWEST taken before a deletion or a --restore, and the very
                 first of those. An unchanged recent backup is reused
                 instead of rewritten
restoring:       --restore-list to see them, --restore <timestamp> to copy one
                 back. It is a rollback: before copying anything it says how
                 many current records are newer than the backup's copy and
                 will be rolled back, and it first backs up the current state
                 of every store it writes into (it does not start if it
                 cannot). It also clears the deletion markers of the sessions
                 it revives, so the next sync will not undo it
visibility:      Claude Desktop reads the session list only at startup and on
                 account switch, so synced sessions show up after a restart
privacy:         the dry run prints your own session titles and profile names;
                 redact it before pasting it into a bug report

duration units:  s=seconds m=minutes h=hours d=days (case-insensitive)
                 examples: --every 20s | 1m | 1m30s | 2h | 30d
"""


def main():
    global QUIET, LOCK_WAIT
    # Session titles and paths can hold any character. Under a non-UTF-8
    # locale (ISO8859-1, US-ASCII, SJIS...) printing one used to raise
    # UnicodeEncodeError and abort the dry run or --restore before it did
    # anything. Files are always written as UTF-8; this only makes the
    # terminal output degrade to escapes instead of crashing.
    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):
        try:
            stream.reconfigure(errors="backslashreplace")
        except (AttributeError, ValueError):
            pass
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="claude-sessions-sync",
        description="Keep the Claude Code session list identical across Claude "
                    "Desktop accounts and profiles.",
        epilog=EPILOG,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--apply", action="store_true",
                    help="apply the changes (without it this is a dry run)")
    ap.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true",
                    help="log only when something changed (used by the background job)")
    ap.add_argument("--propagate-deletions", dest="propagate_deletions",
                    action="store_true",
                    help="opt in to the deletion path: a session whose marker is "
                         "at least as new as the winning record is removed from "
                         "every store and the marker is copied there. Without "
                         "this flag no record or marker is ever unlinked")
    ap.add_argument("--no-delete", dest="no_delete", action="store_true",
                    help="deprecated no-op, kept so existing jobs keep working: "
                         "not propagating deletions is now the default")
    ap.add_argument("--every", "--interval", dest="every", metavar="DURATION",
                    help="background job interval (default 1m). e.g. 20s, 1m, 1m30s, 2h, 30d")
    ap.add_argument("--install", action="store_true",
                    help="install or update the background job")
    ap.add_argument("--uninstall", action="store_true",
                    help="remove the background job")
    ap.add_argument("--status", action="store_true",
                    help="interval, deletion mode, run count, recent log lines")
    ap.add_argument("--wait", type=float, default=0.0, metavar="SECONDS",
                    help="if another sync is running, wait up to this long "
                         "for it instead of giving up at once (default 0, "
                         "capped at 300)")
    ap.add_argument("--restore-list", dest="restore_list", action="store_true",
                    help="list the available automatic backups")
    ap.add_argument("--restore", metavar="TIMESTAMP",
                    help="copy a backup's files back into the matching stores")
    a = ap.parse_args()
    QUIET = a.quiet
    LOCK_WAIT = max(0.0, min(float(a.wait or 0.0), 300.0))
    # OPT-IN. The default may not remove anything, so the flag has to be
    # asked for; --no-delete is only the old name of the default.
    do_delete = bool(a.propagate_deletions)
    if a.no_delete:
        if a.propagate_deletions:
            do_delete = False
            msg = ("note: --no-delete (deprecated, and now the default) wins "
                   "over --propagate-deletions: nothing will be deleted.")
        else:
            msg = ("note: --no-delete is deprecated and does nothing -- not "
                   "propagating deletions is the default now.")
        if not QUIET:
            print(msg)
    # Backups and logs hold session titles and working-directory paths, so
    # everything this process creates stays private to the user.
    os.umask(0o077)

    def run():
        if a.uninstall:
            return uninstall()
        if a.status:
            return status()
        if a.restore_list:
            return restore_list()
        if a.restore:
            with Lock():
                return restore(a.restore)
        if a.install:
            try:
                interval = parse_interval(a.every) if a.every else 60
            except ValueError as e:
                print("error:", e)
                return 1
            return install(interval, do_delete)
        if a.every:
            print("note: --every only means something with --install; "
                  "ignored for a single run.")
        if not os.path.isdir(BASE):
            print("session store not found:", BASE)
            return 1
        with Lock():
            return sync(a.apply, do_delete)

    try:
        return run()
    finally:
        # Unconditional: a run that only ever raises still grows the logs, and
        # that is precisely when the cap has to apply.
        trim_log()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
